 61.\n\n5x + 16y \u2261 5*(30y) + 16y mod 61. 5*30 is 150, so 150y + 16y = 166y mod 61.\n\nNow, compute 166 mod 61. 61*2 = 122, so 166 - 122 = 44. Therefore, 166y \u2261 44y mod 61.\n\nSo, 5x + 16y \u2261 44y mod 61. Now, we need to show that this is not congruent to 0 mod 61. Since y is not divisible by 61, and 44 and 61 are coprime (since 61 is prime and 44 < 61 and 44 is not a multiple of 61), then 44y \u2261 0 mod 61 only if y \u2261 0 mod 61. But y is not divisible by 61, so 44y \u2261 0 mod 61 is impossible. Therefore, 5x + 16y \u2261 44y \u2261 not 0 mod 61. Hence, 5x + 16y is not divisible by 61.\n\nWait, let me check if that's correct. So, the key step is substituting x \u2261 30y mod 61 into 5x +16y, which gives 44y mod 61. Since 44 and 61 are coprime, 44y \u2261 0 mod 61 implies y \u2261 0 mod 61, which contradicts y not being divisible by 61. Therefore, 5x +16y cannot be divisible by 61. That seems right. But let me verify the inverse step again because that's crucial.\n\nWe had 7x \u2261 -34y mod 61. Then multiplied both sides by 35 (the inverse of 7) to get x \u2261 -34*35 y mod 61. Then we calculated 34*35=1190, 1190 mod 61. 61*19=1159, 1190-1159=31. So, 34*35\u226131 mod61, hence -34*35\u2261-31\u226130 mod61. So x\u226130y mod61. Correct.\n\nThen, substituting into 5x +16y gives 5*30y +16y=150y +16y=166y. 166 divided by 61: 61*2=122, 166-122=44. So 166y\u226144y mod61. Since 44 and 61 are coprime (GCD(44,61)=1 because 61 is prime), 44y\u22610 mod61 only if y\u22610 mod61, which is not allowed. Therefore, 44y\u22610 mod61 is impossible, hence 5x +16y\u226144y\u2261not 0 mod61. Therefore, proved.\n\nAlternatively, maybe I can approach this using linear algebra or matrix methods. Let's see. Suppose we have two congruences:\n\n7x +34y \u22610 mod61,\n\nand suppose for contradiction that 5x +16y\u22610 mod61.\n\nThen, we have a system:\n\n7x +34y \u22610 mod61,\n\n5x +16y \u22610 mod61.\n\nWe can set up this system and see if the only solution is x\u2261