 this implies:\n   \\[\n   230y = 61m \\text{ for some integer } m\n   \\]\n\n7. Note the coprimality of 230 and 61:\n   - \\(230 = 2 \\times 5 \\times 23\\)\n   - 61 is a prime number, distinct from factors of 230.\n\n8. Since 230 and 61 are coprime, \\(y\\) must be divisible by 61:\n   \\[\n   y = 61n \\text{ for some integer } n\n   \\]\n\n9. This contradicts the initial condition that \\(y\\) is not divisible by 61.\n\nThus, our assumption that \\(5x + 16y\\) is divisible by 61 must be false.\n\nConclusion:\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{5x + 16y is not divisible by 61}}\n\\]", "domain": "math", "source": "numina_math", "test_cases": null, "starter_code": null}, {"problem": "Let \\( E = \\{1, 2, 3, \\ldots, 200\\} \\) and \\( G = \\{a_{1}, a_{2}, \\ldots, a_{100}\\} \\subset E \\), where \\( G \\) satisfies the following two properties: (1) For any \\( 1 \\leq i \\leq j \\leq 100 \\), \\( a_{i} + a_{j} \\neq 201 \\); (2) \\( a_{1} + a_{2} + \\cdots + a_{100} = 10080 \\). Prove that the number of odd numbers in \\( G \\) is a multiple of 4, and that the sum of the squares of all numbers in \\( G \\) is a fixed value.", "deepseek_reasoning": "Okay, let's try to tackle this problem step by step. The problem is about a subset G of the set E = {1, 2, 3, ..., 200} with 100 elements. The subset G has two properties: first, that no two elements in G add up to 201, and second, that the sum of all elements in G is 10080. We need to prove two things: that the number of odd numbers in G is a multiple of 4, and that the sum of the squares of all numbers in G is a fixed value, meaning it's the same no matter what such subset G we choose.\n\nAlright, let's start by understanding the constraints. The first condition says that for any two elements in G, their sum is not 201. So, G cannot contain both elements of any pair (k, 201 - k) where k is in E. Since E goes up to 200, each pair (k, 201 - k) would consist of distinct numbers except when k = 100.5, but since E only has integers, the p