und of any constant \\( \\alpha \\).\n\n3. **Conclusion**:\n   - Only integers \\( x > 1 \\) have fractional parts \\( \\left\\{ x^n \\right\\} = 0 \\) for all \\( n \\), which satisfies the given inequality with \\( \\alpha = 0 \\).\n   - Non-integer rational numbers fail to meet the condition as their fractional parts oscillate and do not converge to a constant within the required bound.\n\nThus, the \"good\" rational numbers are all integers greater than 1. The final answer is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\mathbb{Z}_{\\geq 2}}\n\\]", "ground_truth_solution": "\nWe are given that a rational number \\( x = \\frac{p}{q} > 1 \\) is considered \"good\" if there exist constants \\( \\alpha \\) and \\( N \\) such that for any integer \\( n \\geqslant N \\),\n\n\\[ \\left|\\{x^n\\} - \\alpha\\right| \\leqslant \\frac{1}{2(p+q)}, \\]\n\nwhere \\(\\{a\\}\\) represents the fractional part of \\(a\\).\n\n1. **Step 1: Understanding the \"good\" condition**\n\n    Each rational number \\( x = \\frac{p}{q} \\) must satisfy the given condition involving \\( \\alpha \\) and \\( N \\). We start by defining \\( m_n = \\left\\lfloor x^{n+1} \\right\\rfloor - \\left\\lfloor x^n \\right\\rfloor \\), where \\(\\left\\lfloor \\cdot \\right\\rfloor\\) denotes the floor function (the greatest integer less than or equal to the number):\n\n    \\[\n    (x - 1)x^n - m_n = \\{x^{n+1}\\} - \\{x^n\\}\n    \\]\n\n2. **Step 2: Applying the \"good\" condition**\n\n    When \\( n \\geqslant N \\), we have:\n\n    \\[\n    \\left|(x - 1)x^n - m_n\\right| \\leqslant \\left|\\{x^{n+1}\\} - \\alpha\\right| + \\left|\\{x^n\\} - \\alpha\\right| \\leqslant \\frac{1}{p+q}\n    \\]\n\n3. **Step 3: Observing the structure of the fractions**\n\n    Note that \\((x - 1)x^n - m_n\\) is a fraction in simplest form with denominator \\( q^{n+1} \\). Therefore,\n\n    \\[\n    \\left|(x - 1)x^n - m_n\\right| < \\frac{1}{p+q} \\quad \\text{for} \\quad n \\geqslant N\n    \\]\n\n4. **Step 4: Simplifying the fraction**\n\n    We can see:\n\n    \\[\n    \\left| q m_{n+1} - p m_n \\right| = \\left| q((x - 1)x^{n+1} - m_{n+1}) - p((x - 1)x^n - m_n) \\right|\n    \\]\n\n    Since each term's absolute value is smaller than \\(\\frac{q}{p+q}\\) and \\(\\frac{p}{p+q}\\), it follows:\n\n    \\[\n    \\left| q m_{n+1} - p m_n \\right| < 1\n    \\]\n\n5. **Step 5: Solving the recurrence**\n\n    Thus,\n\n    \\[\n    m_{n+1} = \\frac{p}{q} m_n \\quad \\text{for} \\quad n \\geqslant N\n    \\]\n\n    Iterating this recursively from \\( n \\) to \\( n+k \\), we have:\n\n    \\[\n    m_{n+k} = \\frac{p^k}{q^k} m_n \\quad \\text{for all} \\quad k\n    \\]\n\n6. **Step 6: General behavior of \\(m_n\\)**\n\n    When \\( n \\) is sufficiently large:\n\n    \\[\n    m_n > (x - 1)x^n - 1 > 0\n    \\]\n\n    From here, we deduce that \\( q = 1 \\) since \\( m_n \\) must remain an integer. Therefore, \n\n    \\[ \n    x > 1 \\quad \\text{is an integer}\n    \\]\n\n### Conclusion\n\nAll \"good\" rational numbers \\( x \\) are integers greater than 1.\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{x \\text{ is an integer and } x > 1}\n\\]", "domain": "math", "source": "numina_math", "test_cases": null, "starter_code": null}, {"problem": "On the base $AC$ of an isosceles triangle $ABC (AB = BC)$, a point $M$ is marked. It is known that $AM = 7$, $MB = 3$, and $\\angle BMC = 60^{\\circ}$. Find the length of the segment $AC$.", "deepseek_reasoning": "Okay, so I need to find the length of segment AC in this isosceles triangle ABC where AB equals BC. Hmm, wait, the problem says ABC is an isosceles triangle with AB = BC. Wait, hold on, if ABC is isosceles with AB = BC, then the base would be AC, right? Because in an isosceles triangle, the two equal sides are the legs, and the unequal side is the base. So if AB = BC, then the base is AC. That makes sense because the problem mentions the base AC. So, vertex B is the apex, and the base is AC. So triangle ABC has AB = BC, base AC, and point M is on AC such that AM = 7, MB = 3, and angle BMC is 60 deg