(B\\):\n   - If \\(a \\geq 1\\), \\(C_U A = \\emptyset\\), so \\((C_U A) \\cap B\\) is empty.\n   - If \\(a < 1\\), \\(C_U A = (a, 2 - a)\\). We need \\((C_U A) \\cap B\\) to have exactly three integers.\n\n4. Analyze the interval \\((a, 2 - a)\\) for \\(a < 1\\):\n   - The interval is symmetric around 1 and must contain exactly three integers.\n   - The integers 0, 1, and 2 must lie within the interval, which requires \\(-1 \\leq a < 0\\).\n\nThus, the possible values of \\(a\\) are \\(\\boxed{[-1, 0)}\\).", "ground_truth_solution": "\n**Problem:**\n\nGiven the universal set \\( U = \\mathbb{R} \\):\n1. Solve the inequality involving \\( x \\): \\( |x - 1| + a - 1 \\geqslant 0 \\) where \\( a \\in \\mathbb{R} \\).\n2. Let \\( A \\) be the solution set of the inequality from part (1). Given the set \\( B = \\left\\{ x \\mid \\sin \\left( \\pi x - \\frac{\\pi}{3} \\right) + \\sqrt{3} \\cos \\left( \\pi x - \\frac{\\pi}{3} \\right) = 0 \\right\\} \\), if \\( \\left( C_U A \\right) \\cap B \\) contains exactly three elements, find the range of values for \\( a \\).\n\n\nPart 1: Solving the Inequality \\( |x-1| + a - 1 \\geqslant 0 \\)\n\n1. We start with the inequality:\n   \\[\n   |x-1| + a - 1 \\geqslant 0\n   \\]\n\n2. Isolate the absolute value term:\n   \\[\n   |x-1| \\geqslant 1 - a\n   \\]\n\n3. Consider two cases for the absolute value expression:\n   - Case 1: \\( x - 1 \\geqslant 1 - a \\):\n     \\[\n     x \\geqslant 2 - a \n     \\]\n   - Case 2: \\( 1 - x \\geqslant 1 - a \\):\n     \\[\n     x \\leqslant a \n     \\]\n\n4. Combine the two cases:\n   \\[\n   x \\leqslant a \\quad \\text{or} \\quad x \\geqslant 2 - a\n   \\]\n\n5. Summarize the solution to the inequality:\n   - If \\( a > 1 \\), \\( 1 - a < 0 \\), every \\( x \\) satisfies the inequality:\n     \\[\n     x \\in \\mathbb{R}\n     \\]\n   - If \\( a \\leq1\\), the solution set is:\n     \\[\n     x \\leqslant a \\quad \\text{or} \\quad x \\geqslant 2 - a\n     \\]\n\nPart 2: Finding the Range of \\( a \\)\n\n1. Define the complement set \\( A \\):\n   - If \\( a > 1 \\):\n     \\[\n     C_U A = \\emptyset\n     \\]\n   - If \\( a \\leq 1 \\):\n     \\[\n     C_U A = \\left\\{ x \\mid a \\leqslant x \\leqslant 2 - a \\right\\}\n     \\]\n\n2. Find the set \\( B \\) from the given trigonometric equation:\n   \\[\n   \\sin \\left( \\pi x - \\frac{\\pi}{3} \\right) + \\sqrt{3} \\cos \\left( \\pi x - \\frac{\\pi}{3} \\right) = 0\n   \\]\n\n   Simplify using trigonometric identities:\n   \\[\n   \\sin \\left( \\pi x - \\frac{\\pi}{3} \\right) + \\sqrt{3} \\cos \\left( \\pi x - \\frac{\\pi}{3} \\right) = 2 \\sin (\\pi x)\n   \\]\n\n   Therefore:\n   \\[\n   2 \\sin (\\pi x) = 0 \\implies \\sin (\\pi x) = 0 \\implies x = k \\quad \\text{for } k \\in \\mathbb{Z}\n   \\]\n\n   Thus:\n   \\[\n   B = \\mathbb{Z}\n   \\]\n\n3. To have \\( \\left( C_U A \\right) \\cap B \\) containing exactly three elements:\n   - These three elements must be integers within the interval \\( \\left[ a, 2 - a \\right] \\).\n\n4. The conditions for three integers \\( n, n+1, n+2 \\) in this interval:\n   \\[\n   a \\leq n \\quad \\text{and} \\quad n+2 \\leq 2 - a\n   \\]\n\n   Therefore:\n   \\[\n   a \\leq n \\quad \\text{and} \\quad n + 2 \\leq 2 - a\n   \\]\n\n5. Combining the inequalities:\n   \\[\n   a \\leq n \\quad \\text{and} \\quad n \\leq 2 - a - 2 \n   \\]\n\n6. Simplify the second inequality:\n   \\[\n   n \\leq 2 - a - 2 = -a \n   \\]\n\n7. Hence \\( a \\) must satisfy:\n   \\[\n   a \\leq -1\n   \\]\n\n### Conclusion:\n\nThus, the range of \\( a \\) is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{a < 1}\n\\]\nThis includes the condition \\( -1 < a \\leq 0 \\) required directly by the constraints of the problem.", "domain": "math", "source": "numina_math", "test_cases": null, "starter_code": null}, {"problem": "1. Using a small calculator, verify that for each integer \\( k = 0, 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7 \\), the sum of the \\( k \\)th powers of each number in the set \\(\\{1, 5, 10, 24, 28, 42, 47, 51\\}\\) is equal to the sum of the \\( k \\)th powers of each number in the set \\(\\{2, 3, 12, 21, 31, 40, 49, 50\\}\\).\n\n2. Let \\( n \\) be a positive integer. Is it possible to find two disjoint sets \\(\\{a_{1}, a_{2}, \\cdots, a_{n}\\}\\) and \\(\\{b_{1}, b_{2}, \\cdots, b_{n}\\}\\), such that the sum of the \\( k \\)th powers of the numbers in the first set is equal to the sum of the \\( k \\)th powers of the numbers in the second set for \\( k = 0, 1, 2, \\cdots, n \\)?", "deepseek_reasoning": "Alright, so I have these two questions here about sets of numbers where their sums of kth powers are equal for various k. Let me try to tackle them one by one. Starting with the first one.\n\n**Question 1: Verification with a Small Calculator**\n\nOkay, the task is to verify for each integer k from 0 to 7 that the sum of the kth powers of each number in the first set {1, 5, 10, 24, 28, 42, 47, 51} is equal to the sum of the kth powers in the second set {2, 3, 12, 21, 31, 40, 49, 50}. Since it's a calculator exercise, I need to compute these sums step by step for each k.\n\nFirst, let me recall that for k=0, any number to the 0th power is 1, so the sum is just the count of numbers in the set. Both sets have 8 elements, so the sum for k=0 should be 8 for both, which is equal. That checks out.\n\nMoving on to k=1. The sum of the first powers is just the sum of the numbers. Let's compute that.\n\nFirst set: 1 + 5 + 10 + 24 + 28 + 42 + 47 + 51. Let me add them step by step:\n\n1 + 5 = 6  \n6 + 10 = 16  \n16 + 24 = 40  \n40 + 28 = 68  \n68 + 42 = 110  \n110 + 47 = 157  \n157 + 51 = 208\n\nSecond set: 2 + 3 + 12 + 21 + 31 + 40 + 49 + 50.\n\n2 + 3 = 5  \n5 + 12 = 17  \n17 + 21 = 38  \n38 + 31 = 69  \n69 + 40 = 109  \n109 + 49 = 158  \n158 + 50 = 208\n\nOkay, both sums are 208. Good, k=1 matches.\n\nk=2: Sum of squares.\n\nFirst set: 1\u00b2 +5\u00b2 +10\u00b2 +24\u00b2 +28\u00b2 +42\u00b2 +47\u00b2 +51\u00b2\n\nCalculating each term:\n\n1, 25, 100, 576, 784, 1764, 2209, 2601.\n\n