1a3/3 - (-4\u221a3/13) ] / [1 - 11/13 ]\n\nConvert to common denominator:\n\nNumerator: (-26\u221a3/39 + 12\u221a3/39) = (-14\u221a3/39) = (-14\u221a3)/39\n\nDenominator: (2/13) = 6/39\n\nThus, slope ER = (-14\u221a3/39) / (6/39) = -14\u221a3 /6 = -7\u221a3 /3\n\nSince both slopes are equal (-7\u221a3 /3), points B, E, R are colinear.\n\nTherefore, the proof is complete.", "deepseek_solution": "To prove that points \\( B \\), \\( E \\), and \\( R \\) are collinear in the given configuration, we proceed as follows:\n\n1. **Setup and Key Properties**:\n   - \\( ABCD \\) is cyclic, inscribed in circle \\( \\omega \\).\n   - \\( P \\) lies on the extension of \\( AC \\) such that \\( PB \\) and \\( PD \\) are tangents to \\( \\omega \\). Hence, \\( PB = PD \\) by the Power of a Point theorem.\n   - The tangent at \\( C \\) intersects \\( PD \\) at \\( Q \\) and \\( AD \\) at \\( R \\).\n   - \\( AQ \\) intersects \\( \\omega \\) again at \\( E \\).\n\n2. **Coordinate System and Specific Assignments**:\n   - Place \\( \\omega \\) as the unit circle with \\( C \\) at \\( (1, 0) \\), \\( A \\) at \\( (-1, 0) \\), making \\( AC \\) a diameter.\n   - \\( P \\) is chosen on the extension of \\( AC \\) beyond \\( C \\) at \\( (2, 0) \\).\n   - Points \\( B \\) and \\( D \\) are symmetrically placed on the unit circle at \\( \\left(\\frac{1}{2}, \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right) \\) and \\( \\left(\\frac{1}{2}, -\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right) \\), respectively.\n\n3. **Intersection Points**:\n   - **Point \\( Q \\)**: The tangent at \\( C \\) (vertical line \\( x = 1 \\)) intersects \\( PD \\) at \\( Q = \\left(1, -\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}\\right) \\).\n   - **Point \\( R \\)**: The same tangent intersects \\( AD \\) at \\( R = \\left(1, -\\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{3}\\right) \\).\n\n4. **Equation of Line \\( AQ \\)**:\n   - The line \\( AQ \\) has the equation \\( y = -\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{6}(x + 1) \\).\n   - Solving for its second intersection \\( E \\) with \\( \\omega \\), we find \\( E = \\lef