   Hence, we have:\n   \\[\n   AB \\cdot DC = BC \\cdot AD\n   \\]\n\n5. **Using Ptolemy\u2019s Theorem:**\n   By Ptolemy\u2019s theorem for the cyclic quadrilateral \\(ABCD\\), we obtain:\n   \\[\n   AB \\cdot DC + AD \\cdot BC = AC \\cdot BD\n   \\]\n   This simplifies our initial ratio condition:\n   \\[\n   AB \\cdot DC = BC \\cdot AD = \\frac{1}{2} CA \\cdot BD\n   \\]\n\n6. **Second Intersection Condition:**\n   Similarly for intersections involving \\(E\\):\n   \\[\n   CA \\cdot ED = CE \\cdot AD = \\frac{1}{2} AE \\cdot DC\n   \\]\n\n7. **Ratios Relation:**\n   We thus have the relations:\n   \\[\n   \\frac{DB}{AB} = \\frac{2DC}{CA} \\quad \\text{and} \\quad \\frac{DC}{CA} = \\frac{2ED}{AE}\n   \\]\n\n8. **Similarity and Intersection Ratios:**\n   From \\( \\triangle RDC \\sim \\triangle RCA \\), we derive:\n   \\[\n   \\frac{RD}{RC} = \\frac{DC}{CA} = \\frac{RC}{RA}\n   \\]\n   Leading to:\n   \\[\n   \\frac{RD}{RA} = \\left( \\frac{RC}{RA} \\right)^2 = \\left( \\frac{DC}{CA} \\right)^2 = \\left( \\frac{2ED}{AE} \\right)^2\n   \\]\n\n9. **Using Similar Triangles involving \\(R\u2019\\):**\n   From \\( \\triangle ABR' \\sim \\triangle EDR' \\), it follows:\n   \\[\n   \\frac{R'D}{R'B} = \\frac{ED}{AB}\n   \\]\n   And from \\( \\triangle DBR' \\sim \\triangle EAR' \\):\n   \\[\n   \\frac{R'A}{R'B} = \\frac{EA}{DB}\n   \\]\n\n10. **Concurrence Proof:**\n    Given:\n    \\[\n    \\frac{R'D}{R'A} = \\frac{ED \\cdot DB}{EA \\cdot AB} = \\left( \\frac{2ED}{AE} \\right)^2\n    \\]\n\n11. **Final Conclusion:**\n    Since the expressions for \\(\\frac{RD}{RA}\\) and \\(\\frac{R'D}{R'A}\\) are the same, it follows that \\(R = R'\\). Therefore, the lines \\(AD\\), \\(BE\\), and \\(CQ\\) are concurrent, and thus, points \\(B\\), \\(E\\), and \\(R\\) are collinear.\n\n\\[\\boxed{\\text{B}, \\text{E}, \\text{R are collinear.}}\\]", "domain": "math", "source": "numina_math", "test_cases": null, "starter_code": null}, {"problem": "Represent the homothety \\( H_{i}^{2} \\) w