3)) because it needs to be the largest number in the entire grid.\n\n2. **Determining the center position possibilities**:\n    - The number in the center (position (2,2)) could be \\(4\\), \\(5\\), or \\(6\\). Each results in different possibilities for the other numbers.\n\nLet's evaluate each case:\n\n#### Case 1: Center = 4\n- With \\(4\\) placed in the center, the numbers \\(2, 3\\) must be positioned around \\(1\\) (the only empty spot next to \\(1\\) is in the same row). So they must fill the positions in the bottom row (positions (3,2),(3,3)).\n- Thus there are \\(\\binom{4}{2}\\) ways to choose positions for \\(7,8\\), and for each choice, there are \\(2!\\) ways to arrange \\(2,3\\) in the remaining positions.\n\\[\n\\text{Total for Center } = 4: \\; \\binom{4}{2} \\times 2! = 6 \\times 2 = 12 \\text{ ways}\n\\]\n\n#### Case 2: Center = 6\n- Following similar reasoning as the previous case but with the numbers \\(7,8\\) arranged in a similar way, we again get:\n\\[\n\\text{Total for Center } = 6: \\; 12 \\text{ ways}\n\\]\n\n#### Case 3: Center = 5\n- Here, the numbers \\(2, 3\\) or \\(2, 4\\) must be adjacent to \\(1\\). Let's evaluate both sub-cases:\n  - **Sub-case 3.1: \\(2\\) and \\(3\\) adjacent to each other**:\n    \\[\n    \\text{Total for Sub-case 3.1}: \\; 4 \\times 1 = 12 \\text{ ways}\n    \\]\n  - **Sub-case 3.2: \\(2\\) and \\(4\\) adjacent to each other**:\n    \\[\n    \\text{Total for Sub-case 3.2}: \\; 3 \\times 1 = 6 \\text{ ways}\n    \\]\n    Summing up both sub-cases:\n\\[\n\\text{Total for Center } = 5: \\; 12 + 6 = 18 \\text{ ways}\n\\]\n#### Summing up all cases: \n\\[\n12 + 12 + 18 = 42 \\text{ different ways}\n\\]\n\n### Conclusion:\n\\[\\boxed{42}\\]", "domain": "math", "source": "numina_math", "test_cases": null, "starter_code": null}, {"problem": "Find the least positive integer \\( n \\) for which there exists a set \\(\\{s_{1}, s_{2}, \\ldots, s_{n}\\}\\) consisting of \\( n \\) distinct positive integers such that\n\\[\n\\