ows (since 3*667=2001, 2003-2001=2). So, in each column, we can place 667 vertical pieces covering 2001 rows, leaving 2 rows uncovered. These remaining 2 rows in each column might be covered by horizontal pieces. But since horizontal pieces can only be placed in a single row and cover 2 columns, maybe we can arrange them to cover those remaining 2 rows across all columns?\n\nWait, but if each column has 2 rows uncovered, then across all 2003 columns, there are 2003*2 = 4006 squares left uncovered. But since horizontal pieces cover 2 squares each, we would need 4006 / 2 = 2003 horizontal pieces. However, horizontal pieces are placed in a single row, so each horizontal piece covers 2 columns in the same row. But here, the uncovered squares are spread across different rows and columns. Let me think again.\n\nActually, each column has 2 rows uncovered. If those 2 rows are the same across all columns, then maybe we can use horizontal pieces in those rows. But since the vertical pieces can be placed anywhere in the columns, the uncovered rows might vary per column. Wait, no. If we use vertical pieces in each column, they will leave the same two rows uncovered in each column. For example, if we place vertical pieces starting from the top, they will cover rows 1-3, 4-6, ..., 2001-2003 (but 2003 is the last row, so maybe the last vertical piece would cover rows 2001-2003). Wait, 3*667=2001, so the first 2001 rows would be covered by vertical pieces, leaving rows 2002 and 2003. But 2003 is the total number of rows, so actually, each column would have vertical pieces covering rows 1-3, 4-6, ..., up to 2001-2003? Wait, no. If the vertical pieces are 1x3, placed vertically, they occupy 3 consecutive rows in a single column. So in column 1, rows 1-3 would be covered by one vertical piece, rows 4-6 by another, etc. But 2003 divided by 3 is 667 with remainder 2. So, after 667 vertical pieces in a column, we would have covered 3*667=2001 rows, leaving rows 2002 and 2003 uncovered. Therefore, each column would have the last two rows (2002 and 2003) uncovered. Wait, but that's only true if we start placing vertical pieces from the top. If we shift the placement, maybe the uncovered rows would be different. However, since all columns are independent in terms of vertical placement, but the problem is that the vertical pieces must be placed vertically along the grid lines. Wait, the problem states that the 1x3 piece can only be placed vertically. So, each vertical piece occupies 3 rows in one column. Therefore, in each column, we can place vertical pieces in positions that cover 3 rows, but since 2003 isn't divisible by 3, there will be two rows left in each column. These two rows must be covered by horizontal pieces. However, horizontal pieces are 1x2, so they cover two columns in the same row. Therefore, if we have two rows left in each column, but they need to be covered by horizontal pieces, which require two columns at a time, we have to check if the arrangement allows tiling those remaining squares.\n\nBut here's the problem: Each column has two rows uncovered. Let's assume they are the same two rows in every column. For example, if all columns have their last two rows (2002 and 2003) uncovered. Then, in rows 2002 and 2003, we have 2003 columns each. To cover these rows with horizontal 1x2 pieces, since each row has 2003 squares, which is odd, we can't tile a single row of 2003 squares with 1x2 pieces, because 2003 is odd. Similarly, each of these two rows is 2003 squares long, which is odd, so they cannot be covered by horizontal pieces alone. Therefore, even if we leave two rows at the bottom, we can't cover them with horizontal pieces. So this approach won't work.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the uncovered rows in each column are different? For example, if we stagger the vertical pieces in such a way that the uncovered rows are spread out across different rows in different columns. However, each vertical piece in a column must be placed startin