inates or barycentric coordinates.\n\nAlternatively, vector approach. Let's try vectors.\n\nLet\u2019s denote vectors for points with position vectors. Let\u2019s take A as origin. So position vectors:\n\nA: 0\n\nB: vector b\n\nC: vector c\n\nThen midpoints:\n\nG: midpoint of BC: (b + c)/2\n\nH: midpoint of CA: (c + 0)/2 = c/2\n\nI: midpoint of AB: (b + 0)/2 = b/2\n\nPoints D, E, F on BC, CA, AB.\n\nLet\u2019s parametrize D, E, F:\n\nLet\u2019s let D divide BC in ratio k:1, so D = (kb + c)/(k + 1)\n\nSimilarly, E divides CA in ratio m:1, so E = (mc + 0)/(m + 1) = mc/(m + 1)\n\nF divides AB in ratio n:1, so F = (n b + 0)/(n + 1) = n b/(n + 1)\n\nBy Ceva's theorem, since AD, BE, CF concur, we have (k/m)(m/n)(n/k) = 1. Wait, Ceva's theorem states that (BD/DC)(CE/EA)(AF/FB) = 1. So BD/DC = k/1, CE/EA = m/1, AF/FB = n/1. Then Ceva's condition is (k)(m)(n) = 1. So kmn = 1.\n\nWait, BD/DC = k/1, so BD = k parts, DC = 1 part. Similarly, CE = m parts, EA = 1 part. AF = n parts, FB = 1 part. Then Ceva\u2019s condition is (k/1)*(m/1)*(n/1) = 1 => kmn = 1.\n\nTherefore, with this parametrization, we have kmn = 1.\n\nNow, lines AD, BE, CF concur at P.\n\nNow, points J, K, L are midpoints of DE, EF, FD.\n\nFirst, let's find coordinates (position vectors) of J, K, L.\n\nPoint J is midpoint of DE.\n\nD = (kb + c)/(k + 1)\n\nE = m c / (m + 1)\n\nSo DE has endpoints D and E. Midpoint J:\n\nJ = [D + E]/2 = [ (kb + c)/(k + 1) + m c/(m + 1) ] / 2\n\nSimilarly, point K is midpoint of EF.\n\nE = m c/(m + 1)\n\nF = n b/(n + 1)\n\nSo EF has endpoints E and F. Midpoint K:\n\nK = [E + F]/2 = [ m c/(m + 1) + n b/(n + 1) ] / 2\n\nPoint L is midpoint of FD.\n\nF = n b/(n + 1)\n\nD = (kb + c)/(k + 1)\n\nMidpoint L:\n\nL = [F + D]/2 = [ n b/(n + 1) + (k b + c)/(k + 1) ] / 2\n\nNow, we need to find equations of lines GK, HL, IJ and show they are concurrent.\n\nFirst, find equations of these lines.\n\nLine GK: connects G and K.\n\nG = (b + c)/2\n\nK = [ m c/(m + 1) + n b/(n + 1) ] / 2\n\nSo directio