 -6b=0 => a -2b=0 ...(6)\n\nFrom (5) and (6):\n\na +2b=0\n\na -2b=0\n\nAdding: 2a=0 => a=0\n\nThen from (5): 0 +2b=0 => b=0\n\nThen c=-a=0\n\nd=1 -b=1\n\nSo the polynomial is P(x)=0x^3 +0x^2 +0x +1 =1. Then the sum is |0| + |0| + |0| + |1|=1, which is much smaller than 7. So this case doesn't give a large sum.\n\nAnother case: suppose P(1)=1, P(-1)=1, P(0.5)=-1, P(-0.5)=-1. Let's see what happens.\n\nEquations:\n\n1. a + b + c + d=1\n\n2. -a + b -c + d=1\n\n3. a/8 + b/4 + c/2 + d=-1\n\n4. -a/8 + b/4 -c/2 + d=-1\n\nAdding 1 and 2:\n\n2b +2d=2 => b + d=1 => d=1 -b\n\nSubtracting 2 from 1:\n\n2a +2c=0 => a +c=0 => c=-a\n\nSubstitute into equation 3:\n\na/8 + b/4 + (-a)/2 +1 -b = -1\n\nMultiply by 8:\n\na +2b -4a +8 -8b = -8\n\n=> -3a -6b +8 =-8\n\n=> -3a -6b =-16 => 3a +6b=16 => a +2b=16/3 ...(5)\n\nEquation 4:\n\n- a/8 + b/4 - (-a)/2 +1 -b =-1\n\nSimplify:\n\n- a/8 + b/4 + a/2 +1 -b =-1\n\nMultiply by 8:\n\n- a +2b +4a +8 -8b =-8\n\n=> 3a -6b +8 =-8\n\n=>3a -6b =-16 => a -2b =-16/3 ...(6)\n\nNow, equations (5) and (6):\n\n(5) a +2b=16/3\n\n(6) a -2b= -16/3\n\nAdding: 2a=0 => a=0\n\nThen from (5):0 +2b=16/3 => b=8/3\n\nFrom c=-a=0\n\nFrom d=1 -b=1 -8/3= -5/3\n\nSo the polynomial is P(x)=0x^3 + (8/3)x^2 +0x -5/3. Then the sum |a| + |b| + |c| + |d|=0 +8/3 +0 +5/3=13/3 \u22484.33, which is still less than 7.\n\nTherefore, the maximum seems to occur when the polynomial alternates between 1 and -1 at the extrema points, which corresponds to the Chebyshev polynomial.\n\nTherefore, the sum |a| + |b| + |c| + |d| cannot exceed 7, and this bound is achieved by the Chebyshev polynomial. Hence, the required inequality is proved.", "deepseek_solution": "To prove that if \\( |P(x)| \\leq 1 \\) for all \\( |x| < 1 \\), then \\( |a| + |b| + |c| + |d| \\leq 7 \\), we utilize properties of polynomials bounded on the interval \\([-1, 1]\\) and the extremal case of the Chebyshev polynomial.\n\n**Step-by-Step Explanation:**\n\n1. **Chebyshev Polynomial Insight:**\n   The third Chebyshev polynomial \\( T_3(x) = 4x^3 - 3x \\) is known to oscillate between \\(-1\\) and \\(1\\) exactly four times within \\([-1, 1]\\). Its coefficients are \\(a = 4\\), \\(b = 0\\), \\(c = -3\\), \\(d = 0\\), yielding a sum \\( |a| + |b| + |c| + |d| = 7 \\).\n\n2. **Key Points Evaluation:**\n   Evaluating \\( P(x) \\) at critical points \\( x = 1, -1, 0.5, -0.5 \\) (where \\( T_3(x) \\) attains extrema) provides constraints:\n   \\[\n   \\begin{cases}\n   |a + b + c + d| \\leq 1, \\\\\n   |-a + b - c + d| \\leq 1, \\\\\n   |\\frac{a}{8} + \\frac{b}{4} + \\frac{c}{2} + d| \\leq 1, \\\\\n   |-\\frac{a}{8} + \\frac{b}{4} - \\frac{c}{2} + d| \\leq 1.\n   \\end{cases}\n   \\]\n\n3. **System of Equations:**\n   Assuming equality at these points (as in \\( T_3(x) \\)):\n   \\[\n   \\begin{aligned}\n   a + b + c + d &= 1, \\\\\n   -a + b - c + d &= -1, \\\\\n   \\frac{a}{8} + \\frac{b}{4} + \\frac{c}{2} + d &= -1, \\\\\n   -\\frac{a}{8} + \\frac{b}{4} - \\frac{c}{2} + d &= 1.\n   \\end{aligned}\n   \\]\n   Solving this system yields \\( a = 4 \\), \\( b = 0 \\), \\( c = -3 \\), \\( d = 0 \\), confirming the Chebyshev polynomial's coefficients.\n\n4. **Maximizing the Sum:**\n   Any deviation from \\( T_3(x) \\)'s coefficients either reduces the sum \\( |a| + |b| + |c| + |d| \\) or violates the \\( |P(x)| \\leq 1 \\) constraint. Testing alternate sign combinations for the constraints results in lower sums, reinforcing that the maximum sum is achieved by \\( T_3(x) \\).\n\n**Conclusion:**\nThe Chebyshev polynomial \\( T_3(x) \\) maximizes the coefficient sum under the given constraint. Thus, \\( |a| + |b| + |c| + |d| \\leq 7 \\).\n\n\\(\\boxed{7}\\)", "ground_truth_solution": "We are given the polynomial $P(x) = ax^3 + bx^2 + cx + d$. We need to prove if for any $|x| < 1$ we have $|P(x)| \\leqslant 1$, then $|a| + |b| + |c| + |d| \\leqslant 7$.\n\n1. **Given Constraints and Initial Condition**:\n    Since \\( P(x) \\) is a continuous function, for any \\( |x| < 1 \\), we have \\( |P(x)| \\leqslant 1 \\). Specifically for \\( x = \\pm 1 \\), the conditions become:\n    \\[\n    |P(\\lambda)| \\leq 1 \\quad \\text{and} \\quad \\left|P\\left(\\frac{\\lambda}{2}\\right)\\right| \\leq 1 \\quad \\text{for} \\ \\lambda = \\pm 1\n    \\]\n\n2. **Choosing Specific Values for \\(x\\)**:\n    For \\( x = \\lambda \\):\n    \\[\n    |P(\\lambda)| = |a\\lambda^3 + b\\lambda^2 + c\\lambda + d| \\leqslant 1 \\implies |\\lambda a + b + \\lambda c + d| \\leqslant 1 \\quad \\text{for} \\ \\lambda = \\pm 1\n    \\]\n   \n    For \\( x = \\frac{\\lambda}{2} \\):\n    \\[\n    \\left|P\\left(\\frac{\\lambda}{2}\\right)\\right| = \\left|a\\left(\\frac{\\lambda}{2}\\right)^3 + b\\left(\\frac{\\lambda}{2}\\right)^2 + c\\left(\\frac{\\lambda}{2}\\right) + d\\right| \\leqslant 1\n    \\]\n    Simplifying the expression on the left,\n    \\[\n    \\left|\\frac{\\lambda a}{8} + \\frac{b}{4} + \\frac{\\lambda c}{2} + d\\right| \\leq 1\n    \\]\n\n3. **Combining and Rearranging Inequalities**:\n    Utilize the values to form combinations:\n    \\[\n    |\\lambda a + b| = \\left|\\frac{4}{3}(\\lambda a + b + \\lambda c + d) - 2 \\left(\\frac{\\lambda a}{8} + \\frac{b}{4} + \\frac{\\lambda c}{2} + d\\right) + \\frac{2}{3}\\left(-\\frac{\\lambda a}{8} + \\frac{b}{4} - \\frac{\\lambda c}{2} + d\\right)\\right|\n    \\]\n    Each term represents an upper bound condition:\n    \\[\n    \\left|\\frac{4}{3}(\\lambda a + b + \\lambda c + d)\\right| + \\left|2 \\left(\\frac{\\lambda a}{8} + \\frac{b}{4} + \\frac{\\lambda c}{2} + d\\right)\\right| + \\left|\\frac{2}{3}\\left(-\\frac{\\lambda a}{8} + \\frac{b}{4} - \\frac{\\lambda c}{2} + d\\right)\\right|\n    \\]\n    Given each term is bounded by 1,\n    \\[\n    \\frac{4}{3} + 2 + \\frac{2}{3} = 4\n    \\]\n    Thus, \n    \\[\n    |\\lambda a + b| \\leq 4 \\quad \\text{and} \\quad |a| + |b| \\leq \\max \\{|a+b|, |-a+b|\\} \\leqslant 4\n    \\]\n\n4. **Repeating for Constants \\(c\\) and \\(d\\)**:\n    Similarly,\n    \\[\n    |\\