\\(nA\\).\n3. **Intermediate Value Theorem**: Ensures a solution exists if \\(n < m < nA\\).\n\nThus, the pairs \\((m, n)\\) must satisfy \\(n < m < nA\\). The final answer is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{(m, n) \\mid n < m < A n \\text{ for positive integers } m, n}\n\\]", "ground_truth_solution": "\n1. Suppose there exists a positive real number \\( x \\) such that \\((1+x)^m = (1+Ax)^n\\).\n\n2. Taking the natural logarithm on both sides, we get:\n   \\[\n   m \\log(1+x) = n \\log(1+ Ax)\n   \\]\n\n3. Rearrange the equation to:\n   \\[\n   \\frac{m}{n} = \\frac{\\log(1 + A x)}{\\log(1 + x)}\n   \\]\n\n4. Consider \\( 1 < 1 + x < 1 + A x \\) because \\( A > 1 \\). Thus:\n   \\[\n   \\log(1 + x) < \\log(1 + A x)\n   \\]\n   and \n   \\[\n   \\frac{\\log(1 + A x)}{\\log(1 + x)} > 1\n   \\]\n\n   Hence, \\( \\frac{m}{n} > 1 \\) which implies \\( m > n \\).\n\n5. Next, assume for contradiction that \\( m \\geq A n \\), and let\u2019s express \\( m \\) as \\( m = A n + \\alpha \\), where \\( \\alpha \\geq 0 \\).\n\n6. Then we have:\n   \\[\n   (1 + x)^{A n + \\alpha} = (1 + A x)^n \n   \\]\n\n7. Rewriting, we have:\n   \\[\n   (1 + x)^{A n} (1 + x)^{\\alpha} = (1 + A x)^n \n   \\]\n\n8. By comparing powers, we would have:\n   \\[\n   (1 + A x)^n \\geq \\left( (1 + x)^A \\right)^n \n   \\]\n   which implies:\n   \\[\n   1 + A x \\geq (1 + x)^A \n   \\]\n\n9. But this inequality does not hold if \\( x > 0 \\) because \\( 1 + A x \\) represents a line tangent to \\( f(x) = (1 + x)^A \\) at \\( x = 0 \\) and \\( f \\) (being a convex function when \\( A > 1 \\)) lies above this line for \\( x > 0 \\).\n\n10. Hence, \\( m < A n \\).\n\n11. To prove the converse, assume \\( n < m < A n \\). Start with:\n    \\[\n    (1+x)^m = (1+A x)^n \n    \\]\n\n12. Equating polynomial expansions:\n    \\[\n    \\sum_{k=0}^{m} \\binom{m}{k} x^k = \\sum_{k=0}^{n} \\binom{n}{k} (A x)^k \n    \\]\n\n13. This simplifies to:\n    \\[\n    \\sum_{k=0}^{n} \\left[ \\binom{n}{k} A^k - \\binom{m}{k} \\right] 