xpressing \\(\\cot \\theta\\) as \\(\\frac{\\cos \\theta}{\\sin \\theta}\\):\n\n\\[\n4 \\cos^2 \\theta = \\frac{\\cos \\theta}{\\sin \\theta}\n\\]\n\nAssuming \\(\\cos \\theta \\neq 0\\), we divide both sides by \\(\\cos \\theta\\):\n\n\\[\n4 \\cos \\theta = \\frac{1}{\\sin \\theta}\n\\]\n\nMultiplying both sides by \\(\\sin \\theta\\):\n\n\\[\n4 \\cos \\theta \\sin \\theta = 1\n\\]\n\nUsing the double angle identity \\(2 \\sin \\theta \\cos \\theta = \\sin 2\\theta\\):\n\n\\[\n2 \\sin 2\\theta = 1\n\\]\n\nThus:\n\n\\[\n\\sin 2\\theta = \\frac{1}{2}\n\\]\n\nSolving for \\(2\\theta\\) within the interval \\([0, \\pi]\\):\n\n\\[\n2\\theta = \\frac{\\pi}{6} \\quad \\text{or} \\quad 2\\theta = \\frac{5\\pi}{6}\n\\]\n\nTherefore, \\(\\theta\\) is:\n\n\\[\n\\theta = \\frac{\\pi}{12} \\quad \\text{or} \\quad \\theta = \\frac{5\\pi}{12}\n\\]\n\nBoth values are acute angles. The correct answer is \\(\\boxed{B}\\).", "ground_truth_solution": "\nFor the equation involving $x$:\n\\[ x^{2} + 4x \\cos \\theta + \\cot \\theta = 0 \\]\n\nWe need to determine the conditions under which this equation has a repeated root (double root).\n\n1. **Identify and Calculate the Discriminant of the Quadratic Equation:**\n\n   A quadratic equation \\( ax^2 + bx + c = 0 \\) has a double root if and only if its discriminant is zero:\n   \\[ \\Delta = b^2 - 4ac = 0 \\]\n   \n   For the given equation \\( x^{2} + 4x \\cos \\theta + \\cot \\theta = 0 \\), we have:\n   \\[ a = 1, \\quad b = 4 \\cos \\theta, \\quad c = \\cot \\theta \\]\n   \n   Therefore, the discriminant is:\n   \\[ \\Delta = (4 \\cos \\theta)^2 - 4 \\cdot 1 \\cdot \\cot \\theta \\]\n   \\[ \\Delta = 16 \\cos^{2} \\theta - 4 \\cot \\theta \\]\n\n2. **Set the Discriminant to Zero and Solve for $\\theta$:**\n   \n   \\[ \\Delta = 16 \\cos^{2} \\theta - 4 \\cot \\theta = 0 \\]\n   \n   Rearrange to isolate $\\cot \\theta$:\n   \\[ 16 \\cos^{2} \\theta = 4 \\cot \\theta \\]\n   \n   Divide both sides by 4:\n   \\[ 4 \\cos^{2} \\theta = 