integral is positive. So that can be directly shown by the neighborhood argument.\n\nI think both parts are covered. The first part via nonnegative Riemann sums leading to nonnegative integral, and the second part using continuity to find a positive lower bound on a subinterval, hence the integral is positive.", "deepseek_solution": "To prove the statements, we proceed as follows:\n\n**1. If \\( f(x) \\geq 0 \\) on \\([a, b]\\), then \\(\\int_{a}^{b} f(x) \\, dx \\geq 0\\):**\n\n**Proof:**  \nSince \\( f \\) is continuous and nonnegative on \\([a, b]\\), every Riemann sum for \\( f \\) over any partition of \\([a, b]\\) consists of nonnegative terms. Specifically, for a partition \\( P = \\{x_0, x_1, \\dots, x_n\\} \\) and sample points \\( c_i \\in [x_{i-1}, x_i] \\), the Riemann sum is:  \n\\[\nS(P, f) = \\sum_{i=1}^n f(c_i) \\Delta x_i,\n\\]  \nwhere \\( \\Delta x_i = x_i - x_{i-1} > 0 \\). Since \\( f(c_i) \\geq 0 \\), each term \\( f(c_i) \\Delta x_i \\geq 0 \\). Thus, \\( S(P, f) \\geq 0 \\). The integral \\( \\int_{a}^{b} f(x) \\, dx \\) is the limit of such Riemann sums as the partition mesh \\( \\|P\\| \\to 0 \\). Since all Riemann sums are nonnegative, their limit is also nonnegative. Therefore:  \n\\[\n\\int_{a}^{b} f(x) \\, dx \\geq 0.\n\\]  \n\n**2. If \\( f(x_0) > 0 \\) for some \\( x_0 \\in [a, b] \\), then \\(\\int_{a}^{b} f(x) \\, dx > 0\\):**\n\n**Proof:**  \nSince \\( f \\) is continuous at \\( x_0 \\) and \\( f(x_0) > 0 \\), there exists \\( \\delta > 0 \\) such that for all \\( x \\) in the interval \\( I = [x_0 - \\delta, x_0 + \\delta] \\cap [a, b] \\), we have:  \n\\[\nf(x) \\geq \\frac{f(x_0)}{2} > 0.\n\\]  \nThe interval \\( I \\) has length \\( L > 0 \\) (adjusting \\( \\delta \\) if \\( x_0 \\) is near \\( a \\) or \\( b \\)). Split the integral over \\([a, b]\\) into two parts:  \n\\[\n\\int_{a}^{b} f(x) \\, dx = \\int_{I} f(x) \\, dx + \\int_{[a, b] \\setminus I} f(x) \\, dx.\n\\]  \n- On \\( I \\), \\( f(x) \\geq \\frac{f(x_0)}{2} \\), so:  \n\\[\n\\int_{I} f(x) \\, dx \\geq \\frac{f(x_0)}{2} \\cdot L > 0.\n\\]  \n- On \\( [a, b] \\setminus I \\), \\( f(x) \\geq 0 \\), so:  \n\\[\n\\int_{[a, b] \\setminus I} f(x) \\, dx \\geq 0.\n\\]  \nCombining these results:  \n\\[\n\\int_{a}^{b} f(x) \\, dx \\geq \\frac{f(x_0)}{2} \\cdot L + 0 > 0.\n\\]  \nThus, the integral is strictly positive.  \n\n**Conclusion:**  \nA continuous nonnegative function on \\([a, b]\\) has a nonnegative integral. If the function is positive at any point in the interval, the integral is strictly positive.  \n\n**Final Answer**  \nThe integral satisfies \\(\\boxed{\\int_{a}^{b} f(x) \\, dx \\geq 0}\\), and if \\( f(x_0) > 0 \\), then \\(\\boxed{\\int_{a}^{b} f(x) \\, dx > 0}\\).", "ground_truth_solution": "\n1. **Initial Assumption**:\n    We are given that the function \\( f(x) \\) is continuous and non-negative on the interval \\([a, b]\\). Specifically, for all \\( x \\in [a, b] \\), we have:\n    \\[\n    f(x) \\geq 0\n    \\]\n\n2. **Considering Non-negative Function**:\n    Since \\( f(x) \\geq 0 \\) on \\([a, b]\\), all Riemann sums used to approximate the integral \\( \\int_a^b f(x) \\, dx \\) will be non-negative. Recall that a Riemann sum for \\( f \\) over \\([a, b]\\) is given by:\n    \\[\n    \\sum_{i=1}^n f(x_i^*) \\Delta x_i\n    \\]\n    where each \\( \\Delta x_i \\) is a subinterval of \\([a, b]\\) and \\( x_i^* \\) is a sample point in \\( \\Delta x_i \\).\n\n3. **Taking Limit of Riemann Sums**:\n    As the function \\( f \\) is continuous on \\([a, b]\\), the integral \\( \\int_a^b f(x) \\, dx \\) is the limit of Riemann sums as the partition gets finer. Formally:\n    \\[\n    \\int_a^b f(x) \\, dx = \\lim_{||P|| \\to 0} \\sum_{i=1}^n f(x_i^*) \\Delta x_i\n    \\]\n    Given that each term in the Riemann sums is non-negative, the integral, which is the limit of these non-negative sums, must also be non-negative. Thus:\n    \\[\n    \\int_a^b f(x) \\, dx \\geq 0\n    \\]\n\n4. **Considering point \\( x_0 \\) where \\( f(x_0) > 0 \\)**:\n    Now, assume there exists a point \\( x_0 \\in (a, b) \\) such that \\( f(x_0) = c > 0 \\). \n\n5. **Ensuring Continuity Around \\( x_0 \\)**:\n    By the continuity of \\( f \\) at \\( x_0 \\), there exists a small interval around \\( x_0 \\) where \\( f \\) remains positive. Specifically, we can find \\( \\delta > 0 \\) such that:\n    \\[\n    f(x) > \\frac{c}{2} \\quad \\text{for} \\quad |x - x_0| < \\delta\n    \\]\n\n6. **Interval Identification**:\n    Choose \\( \\delta \\) small enough so that \\( x_0 - \\delta \\geq a \\) and \\( x_0 + \\delta \\leq b \\). Therefore:\n    \\[\n    \\int_{x_0 - \\delta}^{x_0 + \\delta} f(x) \\, dx > \\int_{x_0 - \\delta}^{x_0 + \\delta} \\frac{c}{2} \\, dx\n    \\]\n    Evaluating the right-hand side:\n    \\[\n    \\int_{x_0 - \\delta}^{x_0 + \\delta} \\frac{c}{2} \\, dx = \\frac{c}{2} \\cdot (x_0 + \\delta - (x_0 - \\delta)) = \\frac{c}{2} \\cdot 2\\delta = c\\delta\n    \\]\n    Therefore:\n    \\[\n    \\int_{x_0 - \\delta}^{x_0 + \\delta} f(x) \\, dx > c\\delta > 0\n    \\]\n\n7. **Integral Decomposition**:\n    We can decompose the integral over \\([a, b]\\) as follows:\n    \\[\n    \\int_a^b f(x) \\, dx = \\int_a^{x_0 - \\delta} f(x) \\, dx + \\int_{x_0 - \\delta}^{x_0 + \\delta} f(x) \\, dx + \\int_{x_0 + \\delta}^b f(x) \\, dx\n    \\]\n\n8. **Non-Negativity of Sub-Intervals**:\n    The integrals over \\([a, x_0 - \\delta]\\) and \\([x_0 + \\delta, b]\\) are non-negative because \\( f(x) \\geq 0 \\) on \\([a, b]\\). Thus:\n    \\[\n    \\int_a^{x_0 - \\delta} f(x) \\, dx \\geq 0 \\quad \\text{and} \\quad \\int_{x_0 + \\delta}^b f(x) \\, dx \\geq 0\n    \\]\n\n9. **Combining Results**:\n    Since both of these integrals are non-negative and we have:\n    \\[\n    \\int_{x_0 - \\delta}^{x_0 + \\delta} f(x) \\, dx > 0\n    \\]\n    it follows that the total integral must be positive:\n    \\[\n    \\int_a^b f(x) \\, dx = \\int_a^{x_0 - \\delta} f(x) \\, dx + \\int_{x_0 - \\delta}^{x_0 + \\delta} f(x) \\, dx + \\int_{x_0 + \\delta}^b f(x) \\, dx > 0\n    \\]\n\n### Conclusion:\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\int_a^b f(x) \\, dx \\geq 0 \\text{ and } \\int_a^b f(x) \\, dx > 0 \\text{ if there exists } x_0 \\in (a, b) \\text{ with } f(x_0) > 0}\n\\]", "domain": "math", "source": "numina_math", "test_cases": null, "starter_code": null}, {"problem": "Given the set \\( S = \\{1, 2, \\cdots, 2005\\} \\), and a subset \\( A \\subseteq S \\) such that the sum of any two numbers in \\( A \\) is not divisible by 117, determine the maximum value of \\( |A| \\).", "deepseek_reasoning": "Okay, so I need to figure out the maximum size of a subset A of the set S = {1, 2, ..., 2005} such that the sum of any two numbers in A isn't divisible by 117. Hmm, let's start by understanding the problem.\n\nFirst, the key condition here is that for any two numbers a and b in A, (a + b) mod 117 \u2260 0. That means if I pick any two numbers from A, their sum shouldn't be a multiple of 117. So my goal is to select as many numbers as possible from S without violating this condition.\n\nSince the problem involves divisibility by 117, maybe working with modulo 117 would help. Let me think: if I categorize numbers in S based on their remainder when divided by 117, then each number can be represented as one of the residues 0, 1, 2, ..., 116. Let's denote these residue classes as R\u2080, R\u2081, R\u2082, ..., R\u2081\u2081\u2086, where R\u2096 = {x \u2208 S | x \u2261 k mod 117}.\n\nNow, if two numbers a and b are in residue classes R_i and R_j, then their sum will be in residue class R_{(i+j) mod 117}. To ensure that their sum isn't divisible by 117, we need to avoid pairs where (i + j) \u2261 0 mod 117. That is, for any two residue classes R_i and R_j, if i + j \u2261 0 mod 117, then we can't have both R_i and R_j represented in A.\n\nSo, the problem reduces to selecting residue classes such that no two classes are complementary modulo 117. Additionally, we have to consider the numbers in ea