{17}}{4} \\right] \\cup [1, \\infty)}\n\\]", "ground_truth_solution": "\nTo solve the inequality \\( 2x^4 + x^2 - 4x - 3x^2 |x-2| + 4 \\geqslant 0 \\):\n\n1. **Rewrite the inequality:**\n\n   Rewrite the inequality in terms of \\( y = |x-2| \\).\n   \\[\n   2x^4 + x^2 - 4x - 3x^2 |x-2| + 4 \\geqslant 0\n   \\]\n   Since \\( y = |x-2| \\),\n   \\[\n   2x^4 + x^2 - 4x - 3x^2 y + 4 \\geqslant 0\n   \\]\n   Simplifying this, we get:\n   \\[\n   2x^4 + x^2 - 4x - 3x^2 y + 4 = 2x^4 + (x-2)^2 - 3x^2 |x-2|\n   \\]\n   Thus,\n   \\[\n   (|x-2|^2 - x^2)((|x-2|^2 - 2x^2) \\geqslant 0\n   \\]\n   Since \\( |x-2| = y \\), we rewrite:\n   \\[\n   (y^2 - x^2)(y^2 - 4x^2) \\geqslant 0\n   \\]\n\n2. **Discriminant Analysis:**\n\n   We represent this as a quadratic:\n   \\[\n   a = 2, \\quad b = -3x^2, \\quad c = x^2, \\quad \\Delta = b^2 - 4ac\n   \\]\n   Calculate the discriminant:\n   \\[\n   \\Delta = (3x^2)^2 - 4 \\cdot 2 \\cdot x^2 = 9x^4 - 8x^4 = x^4\n   \\]\n   Thus, the roots are:\n   \\[\n   y_{1,2} = \\frac{3x^2 \\pm x^2}{2} \\Rightarrow y_1 = x^2 \\quad \\text{and} \\quad y_2 = 2x^2\n   \\]\n\n3. **Rewriting the inequality in terms of \\( y \\):**\n\n   Substitute the roots:\n   \\[\n   (|x-2|- x^2)(|x-2| - 2x^2) \\geqslant 0\n   \\]\n\n4. **Compare with quadratic expressions:**\n\n   \\[\n   (x-2)^2 - x^4 = (x^2 + 4 -4x - x^2) = (x^2 - 2)^2 -x^2\n   \\]\n   Similarly:\n   \\[\n   (x-2)^2 - 4x^4 =  x^2 - 2\n   \\]\n\n5. **Find solution intervals:**\n\n   Solve:\n   \\[\n   (x^2 + x - 2)(x^2 - x + 2)(2x^4 + x - 2) (2x^2 - x + 2) \\geqslant 0\n   \\]\n\n6. **Solving each factor:**\n\n   \\[\n   x^2 + x - 2 = 0 \\Rightarrow (x-1)(x-2) \\Rightarrow x^2 - x + 2 = 0\n   \\]\n\n7. **Identify valid solution intervals:**\n\n   Using these intervals and checking the solution in context, we get:\n   \\[\n   x \\in (-\\infty; -2] \\cup \\left[ \\frac{-1-\\sqrt{17}}{4}; \\frac{-1+\\sqrt{17}}{4} \\right] \\cup [1; \\infty)\n  \\]\n\n8. **Conclusion:**\n\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{(-\\infty ;-