\n    n^2 - 4n + b = 0\n    \\]\n    The discriminant of this quadratic equation is:\n    \\[\n    \\Delta = 4(4-b)\n    \\]\n    Since \\( b \\) and \\( 4 - b \\) are quadratic residues, \\( 4(4 - b) \\) is a nonzero quadratic residue. Hence, there are exactly two distinct values of \\( n \\) that satisfy this equation.\n\n5. **Product Relation:**\n    By pairing the elements \\( n \\) and \\( 4 - n \\) in \\( A \\cup B \\setminus \\{2\\} \\), we obtain:\n    \\[\n    \\prod_{n \\in A \\cup B \\setminus \\{2\\}} n = \\prod_{b \\in B} b\n    \\]\n\n6. **Magic Calculation:**\n    Calculate the product \\( \\prod_{b \\in B} b \\) by evaluating:\n    \\[\n    \\prod_{b \\in B} b = \\prod_{b \\in B} (4 - b) = \\prod_{y \\in \\mathbb{F}_p, \\substack{y \\neq 2 \\\\ 4 - y^2 \\text{ QR}}} (4 - y^2)\n    \\]\n    This translates into:\n    \\[\n    \\prod_{y \\in \\mathbb{F}_p, \\substack{y \\neq 2 \\\\ 4 - y^2 \\text{ QR}}} (2 + y) (2 - y)\n    \\]\n    Combining and simplifications lead to:\n    \\[\n    \\prod_{3 \\leq z \\leq p + 1, \\substack{z \\neq 4, p}} z\n    \\]\n    Note \\( z(4-z) \\) is a nonzero QR if and only if \\( z \\in A \\cup B \\):\n    \\[\n    \\prod_{z \\in \\mathbb{F}_p, \\substack{z \\neq 0, 4, 2}} z \n    \\]\n\n7. **Final Product Adjustment:**\n    Including the missing \\( z = 2 \\) term:\n    \\[\n    \\prod_{b \\in B} b = \\frac{1}{2} \\prod_{a \\in A} a \\prod_{b \\in B} b\n    \\]\n\n8. **Conclusion:**\n    Solving the equation gives:\n    \\[\n    \\prod_{a \\in A} a = 2\n    \\]\n\nThus, the final result of the product of elements of \\( A \\) modulo \\( p \\) is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{2}\n\\]", "domain": "math", "source": "numina_math", "test_cases": null, "starter_code": null}, {"problem": "Prove that any whole number of rubles greater than seven can be paid without change using 3-ruble and 5-ruble banknotes.", "deepseek_reasoning": "Okay, so I need to prove that any whole number of rubles greater than seven can be paid using just 3-ruble and