etic Approach:**\nFor any \\(n \\geq 8\\), analyze \\(n \\mod 3\\):\n1. **If \\(n \\equiv 0 \\mod 3\\):** Use \\(n/3\\) 3-ruble notes.\n2. **If \\(n \\equiv 1 \\mod 3\\):** Use two 5s and \\(\\frac{n - 10}{3}\\) 3s (valid since \\(n \\geq 10\\)).\n3. **If \\(n \\equiv 2 \\mod 3\\):** Use one 5 and \\(\\frac{n - 5}{3}\\) 3s (valid since \\(n \\geq 8\\)).\n\n**Conclusion:**\nBy systematically covering all cases through induction and modular arithmetic, we confirm that every integer \\(n \\geq 8\\) can be expressed as \\(3a + 5b\\) for non-negative integers \\(a\\) and \\(b\\).\n\n**Final Answer:**  \nAny amount greater than 7 rubles can be formed using 3- and 5-ruble notes.  \n\\boxed{\\text{All integers } n \\geq 8 \\text{ can be expressed as } 3a + 5b \\text{ for non-negative integers } a, b.}", "ground_truth_solution": "\n1. **Define the Problem in Mathematical Terms**:\n   We need to show that any whole number amount of rubles \\( x > 7 \\) can be paid exactly using only 3-ruble and 5-ruble bills.\n\n2. **Examine Cases Based on Divisibility by 3**:\n   Since 3 divides any integer \\( x \\) into possible remainders 0, 1, or 2, consider three separate cases:\n\n   - **Case 1**: \\( x \\equiv 0 \\pmod{3} \\)\n   - **Case 2**: \\( x \\equiv 1 \\pmod{3} \\)\n   - **Case 3**: \\( x \\equiv 2 \\pmod{3} \\)\n\n3. **Detailed Solution for Each Case**:\n\n   - **Case 1**: When \\( x \\equiv 0 \\pmod{3} \\)\n     - Here, \\( x = 3m \\) for some integer \\( m \\).\n     - As \\( x > 7 \\), minimum \\( m \\) would be \\(\\lceil 8/3 \\rceil = 3\\), thus \\( x \\geq 9 \\).\n     - Represent \\( x \\) using 3-ruble bills:\n       \\[\n       x = 3m \\quad (\\text{where } m \\text{ is an integer}) \n       \\]\n\n   - **Case 2**: When \\( x \\equiv 1 \\pmod{3} \\)\n     - Here, \\( x = 3m + 1 \\) for some integer \\( m \\).\n     - As \\( x > 7 \\), adjusting for initial values yields \\( x = 5 + 3k \\) for integer \\( k \\geq 1 \\):\n       \\[\n       x = 10 + 3k \\quad (\\text{w