t is dominated by the constant terms \\( 1 \\) at \\( i = 0 \\) and \\( i = n \\), with all other terms vanishing as \\( n \\to \\infty \\). Thus,\n\n$$\n\\boxed{2}\n$$", "ground_truth_solution": "\nTo determine the limit\n\\[\n\\lim_{n \\rightarrow \\infty} \\sum_{i=0}^{n} \\frac{1}{\\binom{n}{i}},\n\\]\nwe start by defining\n\\[\na_n = \\sum_{i=0}^{n} \\binom{n}{i}^{-1}.\n\\]\n\nFor $n \\geq 3$, observe that:\n\\[\na_n = 2 + \\sum_{i=1}^{n-1} \\binom{n}{i}^{-1} > 2.\n\\]\n\nTo gain further insight, analyze the terms and use the binomial coefficient properties. Recall that:\n\\[\n\\binom{n}{i} = \\frac{n!}{i!(n-i)!}.\n\\]\n\nAlso, recall that:\n\\[\n\\binom{n}{0} = 1 \\quad \\text{and} \\quad \\binom{n}{n} = 1.\n\\]\n\nThus:\n\\[\n\\binom{n}{0}^{-1} = 1 \\quad \\text{and} \\quad \\binom{n}{n}^{-1} = 1.\n\\]\n\nWe can express \\( a_n \\) as follows:\n\\[\na_n = 2 + \\sum_{i=1}^{n-1} \\binom{n}{i}^{-1}.\n\\]\n\nNext, take note that for terms where \\(i = 2, 3, \\ldots, n-2\\):\n\\[\n\\binom{n}{i} \\geq \\binom{n}{2}.\n\\]\n\nThis implies:\n\\[\n\\sum_{i=2}^{n-2} \\binom{n}{i}^{-1} \\leq (n-3) \\frac{1}{\\binom{n}{2}}.\n\\]\n\nGiven \\(\\binom{n}{2} = \\frac{n(n-1)}{2} \\), we have:\n\\[\n\\frac{1}{\\binom{n}{2}} = \\frac{2}{n(n-1)} \\leq \\frac{2}{n^2}.\n\\]\n\nHence:\n\\[\n\\sum_{i=2}^{n-2} \\binom{n}{i}^{-1} \\leq (n-3) \\cdot \\frac{2}{n^2} = \\frac{2(n-3)}{n^2} = \\frac{2n - 6}{n^2}.\n\\]\n\nSumming up, we get:\n\\[\na_n \\leq 2 + \\frac{2}{n} + \\frac{2(n-3)}{n^2}.\n\\]\n\nSince \\(\\frac{2(n-3)}{n^2}\\) terms approach 0 as \\(n \\rightarrow \\infty\\):\n\\[\na_n \\leq 2 + \\frac{2}{n} + \\frac{2}{n} \\rightarrow 2.\n\\]\n\nHence, combining both inequalities:\n\\[\n2 < a_n \\leq 2 + \\frac{4}{n}.\n\\]\n\nAs \\( n \\) approaches infinity:\n\\[\n\\frac{4}{n} \\rightarrow 0.\n\\]\n\nThus, the limit is:\n\\[\n\\lim_{n \\rightarrow \\infty} a_n = 2.\n\\]\n\n\\(\\boxed{2}\\)", "domain": "math", "source": "numina_math", "test_cases": null, "starter_code": null}, {"prob