 \\) be the centers of the two circles. Let \\( r \\) and \\( R \\) be the radii of the circles centered at \\( O_1 \\) and \\( O_2 \\) respectively. \n\n2. Let \\( AB \\) and \\( CD \\) be the chords connecting the points of tangency on each circle, and let \\( M \\) and \\( N \\) be the midpoints of these chords respectively. By construction, \\( M \\) and \\( N \\) lie on the line segment \\( O_1 O_2 \\). \n\n3. Consider the right triangles \\( O_2ND \\) and \\( O_2 PO_1 \\). These triangles are similar by the AA (Angle-Angle) similarity criterion, since they both share the angle at \\( O_2 \\), and both contain right angles.\n\n4. Using the similarity of these two triangles:\n\\[ \n\\frac{ND}{O_2D} = \\frac{O_1P}{O_1O_2}\n\\]\n\n5. From the Point of Tangency Theorem, the length of the tangent from a point outside a circle is equal to the square root of the product of the lengths of the segments of any secant line that intersects the circle. Thus, \n\\[\nO_2D = R \\quad \\text{and} \\quad O_1P = r \n\\]\n\n6. Substituting these into the similarity ratio:\n\\[\n\\frac{ND}{R} = \\frac{r}{O_1 O_2}\n\\]\n\n7. Solving for \\( ND \\):\n\\[\nND = \\frac{r \\cdot R}{O_1 O_2}\n\\]\n\n8. Since \\( ND \\) is half of the chord \\( CD \\):\n\\[\nCD = 2 \\cdot ND = \\frac{2 \\cdot r \\cdot R}{O_1 O_2}\n\\]\n\n9. By symmetry, the same argument applies to the chord \\( AB \\):\n\\[\nAB = \\frac{2 \\cdot r \\cdot R}{O_1 O_2}\n\\]\n\n10. Therefore, the lengths of the chords \\( AB \\) and \\( CD \\) are both equal, establishing that:\n\\[\nAB = CD\n\\]\n\n### Conclusion:\n\\[\n\\boxed{AB = CD}\n\\]", "domain": "math", "source": "numina_math", "test_cases": null, "starter_code": null}, {"problem": "For any \\( x \\in \\mathbf{R} \\), \\([x]\\) denotes the greatest integer less than or equal to \\( x \\). Find the set of \\( x \\) that satisfies \\(\\left[\\left|x^{2}-1\\right|\\right]=10\\).\n\n(A) \\((-2 \\sqrt{3}, -\\sqrt{11})\\)\n\n(B) \\([\\sqrt{11}, 2 \\sqrt{3}]\\)\n\n(C) \\((-2 \\sqrt{3}, -\\sqrt{11}] \\cup [\\sqrt{11}, 2 \\sqrt{3})\\)\n\n(D) \\([-2 \\sqrt{3}, -\\sqrt{11}) \\cup (\\sqrt{11}, 2 \\sqrt{3}]\\)", "deepseek_reasoning": "Okay, so I need to solve the equation [[|x\u00b2 - 1|]] = 10, where [.] denotes the greatest integer function, also known as the floor function. The answer choices are given as intervals, so I need to figure out which intervals contain all x such that when you take the absolute value of x\u00b2 - 1 and then apply the floor function, you get 10.\n\nFirst, let me make sure I understand the notation correctly. The double brackets might be a typo or formatting issue, but since the problem mentions [x] as the greatest integer less than or equal to x, I think the equation is intended to be [ |x\u00b2 - 1| ] = 10. So, floor of |x\u00b2 - 1| equals 10. That means |x\u00b2 - 1| is a real number that is greater than or equal to 10 but less than 11. Because the floor function of a number is the greatest integer less than or equal to that number. So if the floor is 10, the actual value must be in [10, 11).\n\nSo, the inequality we need to solve is 10 \u2264 |x\u00b2 - 1| < 11.\n\nNow, let's break that down. |x\u00b2 - 1| is the absolute value of x squared minus 1. So, this inequality splits into two cases:\n\n1. x\u00b2 - 1 \u2265 0, which implies |x\u00b2 - 1| = x\u00b2 - 1. So, in this case, 10 \u2264 x\u00b2 - 1 < 11.\n2. x\u00b2 - 1 < 0, which implies |x\u00b2 - 1| = -(x\u00b2 - 1) = 1 - x\u00b2. So, here, 10 \u2264 1 - x\u00b2 < 11.\n\nLet me handle each case separately.\n\n**Case 1: x\u00b2 - 1 \u2265 0**\n\nThis implies x\u00b2 \u2265 1. So, x \u2264 -1 or x \u2265 1.\n\nThen, the inequality becomes 10 \u2264 x\u00b2 - 1 < 11.\n\nAdding 1 to all parts: 11 \u2264 x\u00b2 < 12.\n\nTherefore, x\u00b2 \u2208 [11, 12).\n\nTaking square roots, since x\u00b2 is in [11,12), x must be in (-\u221a12, -\u221a11] \u222a [\u221a11, \u221a12).\n\nBut \u221a12 can be simplified: \u221a12 = 2\u221a3 \u2248 3.464. Similarly, \u221a11 \u2248 3.316.\n\nSo, in this case, x \u2208 (-2\u221a3, -\u221a11] \u222a [\u221a11, 2\u221a3).\n\nBut we have to remember that in this case, x\u00b2 \u2265 1, so x \u2264 -1 or x \u2265 1. However, since \u221a11 \u2248 3.316 and 2\u221a3 \u2248 3.464, which are both greater than 1, the intervals (-2\u221a3, -\u221a11] and [\u221a11, 2\u221a3) are already subsets of x \u2264 -1 and x \u2265 1, respectively. So, the solution from Case 1 is (-2\u221a3, -\u221a11] \u222a [\u221a11, 2\u221a3).\n\n**Case 2: x\u00b2 - 1 < 0**\n\nThis implies x\u00b2 < 1, so x \u2208 (-1, 1).\n\nHere, the inequality becomes 10 \u2264 1 - x\u00b2 < 11.\n\nLet's solve 10 \u2264 1 - x\u00b2 < 11.\n\nFirst, subtract 1 from all parts: 9 \u2264 -x\u00b2 < 10.\n\nMultiply all parts by -1, which reverses the inequalities: -9 \u2265 x\u00b2 > -10.\n\nBut x\u00b2 is always non-negative, since squaring any real number gives a non-negative result. So, x\u00b2 > -10 is always true, but x\u00b2 \u2264 -9 is impossible because x\u00b2 can't be negative. Therefore, in this case, there are no solutions.\n\nTherefore, Case 2 gives no solutions.\n\n**Combining Both Cases**\n\nThe only solutions come from Case 1, which are (-2\u221a3, -\u221a11] \u222a [\u221a11, 2\u221a3).\n\nLooking at the answer choices:\n\nA) (-2\u221a3, -\u221a11)\n\nB) [\u221a11, 2\u221a3]\n\nC) (-2\u221a3, -\u221a11] \u222a [\u221a11, 2\u221a3)\n\nD) [-2\u221a3, -\u221a11) \u222a (\u221a11, 2\u221a3]\n\nComparing our solution to the options, option C matches exactly. Let me just confirm the endpoints.\n\nIn our solution, for x in (-2\u221a3, -\u221a11], so the left endpoint is open (since x\u00b2 < 12, which is strict inequality) and the right endpoint is closed (since x\u00b2 \u2265 11, which is equality allowed). Similarly, on the positive side, [\u221a11, 2\u221a3) is closed at \u221a11 because x\u00b2 \u226511 and open at 2\u221a3 because x\u00b2 <12. Therefore, the intervals are (-2\u221a3, -\u221a11] and [\u221a11, 2\u221a3), which is exactly option C.\n\nLet me check if any of the other options could be correct. Option D has the endpoints with square brackets on the outer sides: [-2\u221a3, -\u221a11) and (\u221a11, 2\u221a3]. But according to our solution, the inner endpoints (closest to zero) should be closed, and the outer ones should be open. So, for the negative interval, the left endpoint is -2\u221a3, which is open, and the right endpoint is -\u221a11, which is closed. Similarly, the positive interval starts at \u221a11 (closed) and ends at 2\u221a3 (open). So yes, option C is correct.\n\nJust to make sure, let me verify with specific numbers.\n\nTake x = -\u221a11 \u2248 -3.316. Then x\u00b2 = 11. So |x\u00b2 -1| = |11 -1| = 10. Then floor(10) = 10, which satisfies the equation. Therefore, x = -\u221a11 is included, so the interval should be closed there. Similarly, x = \u221a11 \u2248 3.316, x\u00b2 = 11, same result. So [\u221a11 is included.\n\nx = -2\u221a3 \u2248 -3.464. Then x\u00b2 = (2\u221a3)^2 = 4*3 = 12. So |12 -1| = 11. Then floor(11) = 11, which is not 10. Therefore, x = -2\u221a3 is excluded. Similarly, x = 2\u221a3 is \u22483.464, x\u00b2=12, |12-1|=11, floor is 11, which is not 10. Therefore, 2\u221a3 is excluded. So the outer endpoints are open, inner ones closed. Thus, the answer is C.\n\n**Final Answer**\n\\boxed{C}", "deepseek_solution": "To solve the equation \\(\\left\\lfloor \\left| x^2 - 1 \\right| \\right\\rfloor = 10\\), we need to find the set of \\( x \\) such that \\( 10 \\leq \\left| x^2 - 1 \\right| < 11 \\).\n\nThis inequality splits into two cases based on the value of \\( x^2 - 1 \\):\n\n**Case 1: \\( x^2 - 1 \\geq 0 \\)**\n\nThis implies \\( x^2 \\geq 1 \\), so \\( x \\leq -1 \\) or \\( x \\geq 1 \\). The inequality becomes:\n\\[ 10 \\leq x^2 - 1 < 11 \\]\nAdding 1 to all parts:\n\\[ 11 \\leq x^2 < 12 \\]\nTaking square roots:\n\\[ \\sqrt{11} \\leq |x| < \\sqrt{12} \\]\nSince \\( \\sqrt{12} = 2\\sqrt{3} \\), the solutions are:\n\\[ x \\in (-2\\sqrt{3}, -\\sqrt{11}] \\cup [\\sqrt{11}, 2\\sqrt{3}) \\]\n\n**Case 2: \\( x^2 - 1 < 0 \\)**\n\nThis implies \\( x^2 < 1 \\), so \\( x \\in (-1, 1) \\). The inequality becomes:\n\\[ 10 \\leq 1 - x^2 < 11 \\]\nSubtracting 1 from all parts:\n\\[ 9 \\leq -x^2 < 10 \\]\nMultiplying by -1 reverses the inequalities:\n\\[ -9 \\geq x^2 > -10 \\]\nSince \\( x^2 \\) is non-negative, there are no solutions in this case.\n\nCombining the results from both cases, the solution is:\n\\[ (-2\\sqrt{3}, -\\sqrt{11}] \\cup [\\sqrt{11}, 2\\sqrt{3}) \\]\n\nThus, the correct answer is \\(\\boxed{C}\\).", "ground_truth_solution": "\n1. We start with the given condition for any real number \\( x \\):\n   \\[\n   \\left\\lfloor \\left| x^2 - 1 \\right| \\right\\rfloor = 10\n   \\]\n   Here, \\( \\left\\lfloor y \\right\\rfloor \\) denotes the greatest integer less than or equal to \\( y \\).\n\n2. According to the properties of the floor function \\( \\left\\lfloor y \\right\\rfloor \\), we know:\n   \\[\n   10 \\leq \\left| x^2 - 1 \\right| < 11\n   \\]\n\n3. We now analyze two cases based on the sign of \\( x^2 - 1 \\):\n\n   **Case 1: \\( x^2 \\geq 1 \\)**\n\n   For \\( x^2 \\geq 1 \\), we have:\n   \\[\n   \\left| x^2 - 1 \\right| = x^2 - 1\n   \\]\n   Thus, the inequality becomes:\n   \\[\n   10 \\leq x^2 - 1 < 11\n   \\]\n\n   Adding 1 to all parts of the inequality:\n   \\[\n   11 \\leq x^2 < 12\n   \\]\n\n   Taking the square root of all parts, and noting both the positive and negative roots, we get:\n   \\[\n   \\sqrt{11} \\leq |x| < \\sqrt{12} \n   \\]\n   We know:\n   \\[\n   \\sqrt{12} = 2\\sqrt{3}\n   \\]\n   Hence,\n   \\[\n   \\sqrt{11} \\leq |x| < 2\\sqrt{3}\n   \\]\n\n   This gives us two intervals for \\( x \\):\n   \\[\n   \\sqrt{11} \\leq x < 2\\sqrt{3} \\quad \\text{or} \\quad -2\\sqrt{3} < x \\leq -\\sqrt{11}\n   \\]\n  \n   Combining these, we get:\n   \\[\n   -2\\sqrt{3} < x \\leq -\\sqrt{11} \\quad \\cup \\quad \\sqrt{11} \\leq x < 2\\sqrt{3}\n   \\]\n\n4. **Case 2: \\( x^2 < 1 \\)**\n\n   For \\( x^2 < 1 \\), we have:\n   \\[\n   \\left| x^2 - 1 \\right| = 1 - x^2\n   \\]\n   Thus, the inequality becomes:\n   \\[\n   10 \\leq 1 - x^2 < 11\n   \\]\n\n   However, this implies:\n   \\[\n   10 \\leq 1 - x^2\n   \\]\n   Which results in:\n   \\[\n   x^2 \\leq 1 - 10 \\quad \\Rightarrow \\quad x^2 \\leq -9\n   \\]\n   Since \\( x^2 \\leq -9 \\) has no real solution, this case yields no valid \\( x \\).\n\n5. Combining our results from both cases, we conclude that the set of solutions for \\( x \\) is:\n   \\[\n   -2\\sqrt{3} < x \\leq -\\sqrt{11} \\quad \\cup \\quad \\sqrt{11} \\leq x < 2\\sqrt{3}\n   \\]\n\nHence, the correct choice is \n\\(\n\\text{(C)}\n\\).\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{(-2 \\sqrt{3}, -\\sqrt{11}] \\cup [\\sqrt{11}, 2 \\sqrt{3})}\n\\]", "domain": "math", "source": "numina_math", "test_cases": null, "starter_code": null}, {"problem": "A worker supervises three machines. Within one hour, the probabilities that machine A, machine B, and machine C need supervision are $0.9$, $0.8$, and $0.85$, respectively. Within one hour, find:\n(1) The probability that none of the machines need supervision;\n(2) The probability that at least one machine needs supervision.", "deepseek_reasoning": "Okay, let's tackle these probability problems step by step. So, the problem is about a worker supervising three machines\u2014A, B, and C. The probabilities that each machine needs supervision within an hour are 0.9, 0.8, and 0.85 respectively. We need to find two probabilities: first, the probability that none of the machines need supervision, and second, the probability that at least one machine needs supervision.\n\nAlright, starting with part (1), the probability that none of the machines need supervision. Hmm. Let me recall my probability basics. If the probability that an event happens is p, then the probability that it doesn't happen is 1 - p. So, since we want none of the machines to need supervision, we need the probability that machine A doesn't need supervision, machine B doesn't, and machine C doesn't. Since these are independent events\u2014right? The problem doesn't state any dependence between the machines needing supervision, so I think it's safe to assume they're independent.\n\nSo, if the probability that machine A needs supervision is 0.9, then the probability it doesn't need supervision is 1 - 0.9 = 0.1. Similarly, for machine B, it's 1 - 0.8 = 0.2, and for machine C, 1 - 0.85 = 0.15. Since the events are independent, we can multiply these probabilities together to get the probability that all three don't need supervision at the same time. So that would be 0.1 * 0.2 * 0.15. Let me compute that. 0.1 times 0.2 is 0.02, and then 0.02 times 0.15. Hmm, 0.02 * 0.15 is 0.003. So, 0.003 is the probability that none of the machines need supervision. That seems low, but considering each machine has a high probability of needing supervision individually, the chance that all three don't need it would indeed be very small. Let me double-check the calculations. 0.1 * 0.2 is 0.02, correct. Then 0.02 * 0.15: 0.02 * 0.1 is 0.002, and 0.02 * 0.05 is 0.001, so adding those together gives 0.003. Yes, that's right. So part (1) should be 0.003.\n\nNow moving on to part (2), the probability that at least one machine needs supervision. Hmm, \"at least one\" is a common probability scenario. I remember that the complement of \"at least one\" is \"none.\" So instead of calculating the probability of at least one machine needing supervision directly, which could be complicated because there are multiple cases (exactly one, exactly two, exactly three), we can use the complement rule. The probability of at least one is equal to 1 minus the probability that none of them need supervision. Wait, that's exactly what we calculated in part (1). So if part (1) is 0.003, then part (2) should be 1 - 0.003 = 0.997. That makes sense because if there's a 0.3% chance none need supervision, there's a 99.7% chance that at least one does. Let me verify that this approach is correct.\n\nYes, in probability, when dealing with \"at least one,\" using the complement rule is a standard approach. Since calculating the probability of none is straightforward, especially with independent events, subtracting that from 1 gives the desired result. So part (2) is indeed 0.997.\n\nBut just to be thorough, let's consider the alternative approach where we calculate the probability of at least one by adding the probabilities of exactly one, exactly two, and exactly three machines needing supervision. Let's see if that gives the same result. \n\nFirst, the probability that exactly one machine needs supervision. That would be the sum of the probabilities that A needs and B and C don't, plus B needs and A and C don't, plus C needs and A and B don't. Let's compute each:\n\n- A needs, others don't: 0.9 * (1 - 0.8) * (1 - 0.85) = 0.9 * 0.2 * 0.15 = 0.027\n- B needs, others don't: (1 - 0.9) * 0.8 * (1 - 0.85) = 0.1 * 0.8 * 0.15 = 0.012\n- C needs, others don't: (1 - 0.9) * (1 - 0.8) * 0.85 = 0.1 * 0.2 * 0.85 = 0.017\n\nAdding these together: 0.027 + 0.012 + 0.017 = 0.056\n\nNext, the probability that exactly two machines need supervision:\n\n- A and B need, C doesn't: 0.9 * 0.8 * (1 - 0.85) = 0.9 * 0.8 * 0.15 = 0.108\n- A and C need, B doesn't: 0.9 * (1 - 0.8) * 0.85 = 0.9 * 0.2 * 0.85 = 0.153\n- B and C need, A doesn't: (1 - 0.9) * 0.8 * 0.85 = 0.1 * 0.8 * 0.85 = 0.068\n\nAdding these: 0.108 + 0.153 + 0.068 = 0.329\n\nThen, the probability that all three need supervision: 0.9 * 0.8 * 0.85. Let's compute that:\n\n0.9 * 0.8 = 0.72; 0.72 * 0.85. Let's calculate 0.72 * 0.8 = 0.576, and 0.72 * 0.05 = 0.036, so total is 0.576 + 0.036 = 0.612.\n\nNow, adding up all three cases: exactly one (0.056) + exactly two (0.329) + exactly three (0.612) = 0.056 + 0.329 = 0.385; 0.385 + 0.612 = 0.997. \n\nSo that matches the result from the complement method. Therefore, part (2) is indeed 0.997. This cross-verification assures me that the answer is correct.\n\nBut let me just check if there's another way to think about it. For example, using inclusion-exclusion principle. The probability of at least one is P(A \u222a B \u222a C) = P(A) + P(B) + P(C) - P(A \u2229 B) - P(A \u2229 C) - P(B \u2229 C) + P(A \u2229 B \u2229 C). Let's compute that.\n\nGiven P(A) = 0.9, P(B) = 0.8, P(C) = 0.85.\n\nFirst, sum of individual probabilities: 0.9 + 0.8 + 0.85 = 2.55\n\nThen subtract the pairwise intersections. The probabilities of two machines needing supervision:\n\nP(A \u2229 B) = 0.9 * 0.8 = 0.72\n\nP(A \u2229 C) = 0.9 * 0.85 = 0.765\n\nP(B \u2229 C) = 0.8 * 0.85 = 0.68\n\nSum of pairwise intersections: 0.72 + 0.765 + 0.68 = 2.165\n\nThen add back the probability of all three: P(A \u2229 B \u2229 C) = 0.9 * 0.8 * 0.85 = 0.612 as before.\n\nSo applying inclusion-exclusion:\n\nP(A \u222a B \u222a C) = 2.55 - 2.165 + 0.612 = (2.55 - 2.165) = 0.385; 0.385 + 0.612 = 0.997. Again, same result. So regardless of the method, we get 0.997 for part (2). That gives me even more confidence.\n\nBut let me just ensure that all steps make sense. When we use inclusion-exclusion, we are accounting for overlaps. Since adding the individual probabilities counts the overlaps twice, so we subtract them once, but then subtract too much, so we add back the triple overlap. That makes sense.\n\nAlternatively, considering that each machine has a high probability, the chance that at least one needs supervision is extremely high, which aligns with 0.997.\n\nSo, summarizing:\n\n1. Probability none need supervision: 0.1 * 0.2 * 0.15 = 0.003\n\n2. Probability at least one needs supervision: 1 - 0.003 = 0.997\n\nAll cross-verification methods lead to the same answers, so I think these are correct. I don't see any errors in the reasoning here. The key was recognizing the independence of the events and appropriately applying the complement rule for the second part. Also, confirming with alternative methods like inclusion-exclusion and direct calculation of all possible cases adds robustness to the solution.\n\n**Final Answer**\n\n(1) The probability that none of the machines need supervision is \\boxed{0.003}.\n\n(2) The probability that at least one machine needs supervision is \\boxed{0.997}.", "deepseek_solution": "To find the probabilities for the given problem, we start by considering the probabilities that each machine does not need supervision. \n\n1. **Probability that none of the machines need supervision:**\n\n   - The probability that machine A does not need supervision is \\(1 - 0.9 = 0.1\\).\n   - The probability that machine B does not need supervision is \\(1 - 0.8 = 0.2\\).\n   - The probability that machine C does not need supervision is \\(1 - 0.85 = 0.15\\).\n\n   Since the events are independent, we multiply these probabilities:\n   \\[\n   0.1 \\times 0.2 \\times 0.15 = 0.003\n   \\]\n\n2. **Probability that at least one machine needs supervision:**\n\n   Using the complement rule, we subtract the probability that none of the machines need supervision from 1:\n   \\[\n   1 - 0.003 = 0.997\n   \\]\n\n   To verify, we can use the inclusion-exclusion principle or calculate the probabilities of all cases (exactly one, two, or three machines needing supervision) and sum them up. Both methods confirm that the result is consistent with the complement rule.\n\n### Final Answer\n\n(1) The probability that none of the machines need supervision is \\boxed{0.003}.\n\n(2) The probability that at least one machine needs supervision is \\boxed{0.997}.", "ground_truth_solution": "\nLet's define the following events:\n- \\(A\\): Machine \\(\\alpha\\) needs attention within one hour.\n- \\(B\\): Machine \\(\\beta\\) needs attention within one hour.\n- \\(C\\): Machine \\(\\gamma\\) needs attention within one hour.\n\nThe probabilities given are:\n\\[\nP(A) = 0.9, \\quad P(B) = 0.8, \\quad P(C) = 0.85\n\\]\n\nSince \\( \\bar{A}, \\bar{B}, \\bar{C} \\) represent the probabilities that the corresponding machines do not need attention, we have:\n\\[\nP(\\bar{A}) = 1 - P(A) = 0.1, \\quad P(\\bar{B}) = 1 - P(B) = 0.2, \\quad P(\\bar{C}) = 1 - P(C) = 0.15\n\\]\n\n#### (1) The probability that none of the machines need attention:\nWe need to find the probability of the event \\( \\bar{A} \\cap \\bar{B} \\cap \\bar{C} \\). Since the events are independent:\n\\[\nP(\\bar{A} \\cap \\bar{B} \\cap \\bar{C}) = P(\\bar{A}) \\cdot P(\\bar{B}) \\cdot P(\\bar{C})\n\\]\n\nSubstituting the values:\n\\[\nP(\\bar{A} \\cap \\bar{B} \\cap \\bar{C}) = 0.1 \\cdot 0.2 \\cdot 0.15 = 0.003\n\\]\n\nThus, the probability that none of the machines need attention is:\n\\[\nP(\\text{None need attention}) = 0.003\n\\]\n\n#### (2) The probability that at least one machine needs attention:\nLet \\( D \\) be the event that at least one machine needs attention. By the complement rule:\n\\[\nP(D) = 1 - P(\\bar{A} \\cap \\bar{B} \\cap \\bar{C})\n\\]\n\nSubstituting the value from (1):\n\\[\nP(D) = 1 - 0.003 = 0.997\n\\]\n\nThus, the probability that at least one machine needs attention is:\n\\[\nP(\\text{At least one needs attention}) = 0.997\n\\]\n\n### Conclusion:\nThe final answers are:\n1. The probability that none of the machines need attention is:\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{0.003}\n   \\]\n2. The probability that at least one machine needs attention is:\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{0.997}\n   \\]", "domain": "math", "source": "numina_math", "test_cases": null, "starter_code": null}, {"problem": "There are 100 sticks, from which a 100-sided polygon can be formed.\n\nIs it possible that a polygon cannot be formed from any smaller number of these sticks?", "deepseek_reasoning": "Okay, let's try to figure out this problem. So, we have 100 sticks, and we can form a 100-sided polygon with them. The question is asking if it's possible that you can't form a polygon using any smaller number of these sticks. Hmm, interesting. So, basically, can there be a set of 100 sticks where a 100-gon is possible, but no k-gon is pos