ed to find where this bisector intersects the circle \u0393 again at point C. The circle \u0393 has equation x\u00b2 + y\u00b2 = r\u00b2.\n\nSubstituting x and y from the parametric equations into the circle equation:\n\n(r + t*(-1 - sin(\u03b8/2)))\u00b2 + (t*cos(\u03b8/2))\u00b2 = r\u00b2.\n\nExpand this:\n\n[r - t(1 + sin(\u03b8/2))]^2 + [t cos(\u03b8/2)]^2 = r\u00b2\n\nExpanding the first term:\n\nr\u00b2 - 2 r t (1 + sin(\u03b8/2)) + t\u00b2 (1 + sin(\u03b8/2))\u00b2 + t\u00b2 cos\u00b2(\u03b8/2) = r\u00b2\n\nSubtract r\u00b2 from both sides:\n\n-2 r t (1 + sin(\u03b8/2)) + t\u00b2 [ (1 + sin(\u03b8/2))\u00b2 + cos\u00b2(\u03b8/2) ] = 0\n\nFactor out t:\n\nt [ -2 r (1 + sin(\u03b8/2)) + t ( (1 + sin(\u03b8/2))\u00b2 + cos\u00b2(\u03b8/2) ) ] = 0\n\nSolutions are t = 0 (which is point A) and:\n\nt = [ 2 r (1 + sin(\u03b8/2)) ] / [ (1 + sin(\u03b8/2))\u00b2 + cos\u00b2(\u03b8/2) ]\n\nSimplify the denominator:\n\n(1 + sin(\u03b8/2))\u00b2 + cos\u00b2(\u03b8/2) = 1 + 2 sin(\u03b8/2) + sin\u00b2(\u03b8/2) + cos\u00b2(\u03b8/2) = 2 + 2 sin(\u03b8/2)\n\nTherefore, t = [ 2 r (1 + sin(\u03b8/2)) ] / [ 2 + 2 sin(\u03b8/2) ] = [ 2 r (1 + sin(\u03b8/2)) ] / [ 2 (1 + sin(\u03b8/2)) ] = r.\n\nSo, t = r. Therefore, the coordinates of point C are:\n\nx = r + r*(-1 - sin(\u03b8/2)) = r - r - r sin(\u03b8/2) = - r sin(\u03b8/2)\n\ny = 0 + r*cos(\u03b8/2) = r cos(\u03b8/2)\n\nTherefore, point C has coordinates (- r sin(\u03b8/2), r cos(\u03b8/2)).\n\nInteresting. Let me verify that point C lies on \u0393:\n\nx\u00b2 + y\u00b2 = [ - r sin(\u03b8/2) ]\u00b2 + [ r cos(\u03b8/2) ]\u00b2 = r\u00b2 sin\u00b2(\u03b8/2) + r\u00b2 cos\u00b2(\u03b8/2) = r\u00b2 (sin\u00b2(\u03b8/2) + cos\u00b2(\u03b8/2)) = r\u00b2. Yes, it does.\n\nSo, point C is at (- r sin(\u03b8/2), r cos(\u03b8/2)). Let me note that this is equivalent to (r cos( \u03c0/2 + \u03b8/2 ), r sin( \u03c0/2 + \u03b8/2 )), which is a rotation of \u03b8/2 + \u03c0/2 from the positive x-axis. Alternatively, since sin(\u03b8/2) =