 D + sin \u03b8 E = -r --> (2)\n\nLet me use matrix notation:\n\n[ -sin(\u03b8/2)  cos(\u03b8/2) ] [ D ]   = [ -r ]\n\n[  cos \u03b8      sin \u03b8   ] [ E ]     [ -r ]\n\nCompute the determinant of the coefficient matrix:\n\n\u0394 = (-sin(\u03b8/2))(sin \u03b8) - cos(\u03b8/2)(cos \u03b8) = - sin(\u03b8/2) sin \u03b8 - cos(\u03b8/2) cos \u03b8\n\nUsing trigonometric identities, we can simplify:\n\nRecall that cos(A + B) = cos A cos B - sin A sin B\n\nBut here, we have - [ sin(\u03b8/2) sin \u03b8 + cos(\u03b8/2) cos \u03b8 ] = - [ cos(\u03b8/2) cos \u03b8 + sin(\u03b8/2) sin \u03b8 ] = - cos(\u03b8 - \u03b8/2) = - cos(\u03b8/2)\n\nBecause cos(A - B) = cos A cos B + sin A sin B. So if we set A = \u03b8, B = \u03b8/2, then cos(\u03b8 - \u03b8/2) = cos(\u03b8/2). Therefore:\n\n\u0394 = - cos(\u03b8/2)\n\nAssuming \u0394 \u2260 0, which requires \u03b8/2 \u2260 90\u00b0, so \u03b8 \u2260 180\u00b0, which is acceptable since \u03b8 > 60\u00b0 and less than 180\u00b0, so \u03b8/2 ranges from 30\u00b0 to 90\u00b0, so cos(\u03b8/2) \u2260 0. Therefore, the determinant is non-zero, so the system has a unique solution.\n\nUsing Cramer's Rule:\n\nD = \u0394_D / \u0394\n\nE = \u0394_E / \u0394\n\nWhere \u0394_D is the determinant formed by replacing the first column with the constants:\n\n\u0394_D = | -r  cos(\u03b8/2) |\n\n          | -r   sin \u03b8     |\n\n= (-r)(sin \u03b8) - (-r)(cos(\u03b8/2)) = -r sin \u03b8 + r cos(\u03b8/2)\n\nSimilarly, \u0394_E is the determinant formed by replacing the second column:\n\n\u0394_E = | -sin(\u03b8/2)  -r |\n\n          |  cos \u03b8      -r |\n\n= (-sin(\u03b8/2))(-r) - (cos \u03b8)(-r) = r sin(\u03b8/2) + r cos \u03b8\n\nTherefore,\n\nD = ( -r sin \u03b8 + r cos(\u03b8/2) ) / ( - cos(\u03b8/2) ) = [ r ( - sin \u03b8 + cos(\u03b8/2) ) ] / ( - cos(\u03b8/2) ) = [ r ( cos(\u03b8/2) - sin \u03b8 ) ] / ( - cos(\u03b8/2) ) = r ( sin \u03b8 - cos(\u03b8/2) ) / cos(\u03b8/2)\n\nSimilarly,\n\nE = ( r sin(\u03b8/2) + r cos \u03b8 ) / ( - cos(\u03b8/2) ) = r ( sin(\u03b8/2) + cos \u03b8 ) / ( - cos(\u03b8/2) ) = - r ( sin(\u03b8/2) + cos \u03b8 ) / cos(\u03b8/2 )\n\nSimplify D and E:\n\nD = r [ sin \u03b8 - cos(\u03b8/2) ] / cos(\u03b8/2 ) = r [ sin \u03b8 / cos(\u03b8/2) - 1 ]\n\nE = - r [ sin(\u03b8/2) + cos \u03b8 ] / cos(\u03b8/2 ) = - r [ sin(\u03b8/2)/cos(\u03b8/2) + cos \u03b8 / cos(\u03b8/2) ] = - r [ tan(\u03b8/2) + cos \u03b8 / cos(\u03b8/2) ]\n\nHmm, this is getting complicated. Maybe we can express sin \u03b8 and cos \u03b8 in terms of \u03b8/2.\n\nRecall that:\n\nsin \u03b8 = 2 sin(\u03b8/2) cos(\u03b8/2)\n\ncos \u03b8 = 2 cos\u00b2(\u03b8/2) - 1\n\nSo, substitute these into D and E:\n\nD = r [ 2 sin(\u03b8/2) cos(\u03b8/2) / cos(\u03b8/2) - 1 ] = r [ 2 sin(\u03b8/2) - 1 ]\n\nSimilarly, for E:\n\nE = - r [ tan(\u03b8/2) + (2 cos\u00b2(\u03b8/2) - 1) / cos(\u03b8/2) ] = - r [ tan(\u03b8/2) + 2 cos(\u03b8/2) - sec(\u03b8/2) ]\n\nWait, let's compute term by term:\n\ncos \u03b8 / cos(\u03b8/2) = [ 2 cos\u00b2(\u03b8/2) - 1 ] / cos(\u03b8/2) = 2 cos(\u03b8/2) - sec(\u03b8/2)\n\nTherefore,\n\nE = - r [ tan(\u03b8/2) + 2 cos(\u03b8/2) - sec(\u03b8/2) ] = - r [ (sin(\u03b8/2)/cos(\u03b8/2)) + 2 cos(\u03b8/2) - 1/cos(\u03b8/2) ]\n\nCombine terms:\n\n= - r [ (sin(\u03b8/2) - 1)/cos(\u03b8/2) + 2 cos(\u03b8/2) ]\n\nHmm, not sure if this helps. Maybe keep D and E as they are.\n\nSo, the equation of circle \u03a9 is x\u00b2 + y\u00b2 + D x + E y = 0, where D and E are expressed in terms of \u03b8.\n\nNow, we need to find the second intersection point D of \u03a9 with line AB, other than B.\n\nLine AB goes from A(r, 0) to B(r cos \u03b8, r sin \u03b8). Parametrize line AB.\n\nParametric equations for line AB can be written as:\n\nx = r + t (r cos \u03b8 - r) = r (1 + t (cos \u03b8 - 1))\n\ny = 0 + t (r sin \u03b8 - 0) = r t sin \u03b8\n\nWhere t ranges from 0 to 1 for the segment AB, but since D could be outside AB, t can be any real number.\n\nWe need to find the points where this line intersects circle \u03a9: x\u00b2 + y\u00b2 + D x + E y = 0.\n\nSubstitute x and y from the parametric equations into the circle equation:\n\n[ r (1 + t (cos \u03b8 - 1)) ]\u00b2 + [ r t sin \u03b8 ]\u00b2 + D [ r (1 + t (cos \u03b8 - 1)) ] + E [ r t sin \u03b8 ] = 0\n\nDivide both sides by r\u00b2:\n\n[ (1 + t (cos \u03b8 - 1))\u00b2 + t\u00b2 sin\u00b2 \u03b8 ] + (D/r) [1 + t (cos \u03b8 - 1) ] + (E/r) t sin \u03b8 = 0\n\nLet me compute each term:\n\nFirst term: (1 + t (cos \u03b8 - 1))\u00b2 + t\u00b2 sin\u00b2 \u03b8\n\nExpand the first square:\n\n= 1 + 2 t (cos \u03b8 - 1) + t\u00b2 (cos \u03b8 - 1)\u00b2 + t\u00b2 sin\u00b2 \u03b8\n\nCombine the t\u00b2 terms:\n\n= 1 + 2 t (cos \u03b8 - 1) + t\u00b2 [ (cos \u03b8 - 1)^2 + sin\u00b2 \u03b8 ]\n\nExpand (cos \u03b8 - 1)^2:\n\n= cos\u00b2 \u03b8 - 2 cos \u03b8 + 1\n\nSo,\n\n(cos \u03b8 - 1)^2 + sin\u00b2 \u03b8 = cos\u00b2 \u03b8 - 2 cos \u03b8 + 1 + sin\u00b2 \u03b8 = (cos\u00b2 \u03b8 + sin\u00b2 \u03b8) - 2 cos \u03b8 + 1 = 1 - 2 cos \u03b8 + 1 = 2(1 - cos \u03b8)\n\nTherefore, first term becomes:\n\n1 + 2 t (cos \u03b8 - 1) + 2 t\u00b2 (1 - cos \u03b8)\n\nNow, the second term:\n\n(D/r)[1 + t (cos \u03b8 - 1) ] = (D/r) + (D/r) t (cos \u03b8 - 1 )\n\nThird term:\n\n(E/r) t sin \u03b8\n\nPutting it all together:\n\n[1 + 2 t (cos \u03b8 - 1) + 2 t\u00b2 (1 - cos \u03b8)] + (D/r) + (D/r) t (cos \u03b8 - 1 ) + (E/r) t sin \u03b8 = 0\n\nCombine constants:\n\n1 + (D/r)\n\nLinear terms in t:\n\n2 t (cos \u03b8 - 1) + (D/r) t (cos \u03b8 - 1) + (E/r) t sin \u03b8\n\nQuadratic term:\n\n2 t\u00b2 (1 - cos \u03b8)\n\nSo, the equation is:\n\n2(1 - cos \u03b8) t\u00b2 + [ 2 (cos \u03b8 - 1) + (D/r)(cos \u03b8 - 1) + (E/r) sin \u03b8 ] t + 1 + (D/r) = 0\n\nWe know that point B corresponds to t = 1, since when t = 1:\n\nx = r(1 + (cos \u03b8 - 1)) = r cos \u03b8\n\ny = r sin \u03b8\n\nWhich is point B. So, t = 1 is a root of this equation. Therefore, we can factor out (t - 1).\n\nLet me denote the quadratic equation as:\n\nA t\u00b2 + B t + C = 0\n\nWhere:\n\nA = 2(1 - cos \u03b8)\n\nB = 2 (cos \u03b8 - 1) + (D/r)(cos \u03b8 - 1) + (E/r) sin \u03b8\n\nC = 1 + (D/r)\n\nSince t = 1 is a root, then A + B + C = 0.\n\nLet me check:\n\nA + B + C = 2(1 - cos \u03b8) + [ 2 (cos \u03b8 - 1) + (D/r)(cos \u03b8 - 1) + (E/r) sin \u03b8 ] + [ 1 + (D/r) ]\n\nSimplify term by term:\n\n= 2 - 2 cos \u03b8 + 2 cos \u03b8 - 2 + (D/r)(cos \u03b8 - 1) + (E/r) sin \u03b8 + 1 + (D/r)\n\n= [2 - 2] + [ -2 cos \u03b8 + 2 cos \u03b8 ] + (D/r)(cos \u03b8 - 1 + 1) + (E/r) sin \u03b8 + 1\n\n= 0 + 0 + (D/r) cos \u03b8 + (E/r) sin \u03b8 + 1\n\nFrom equation (2):\n\nr + D cos \u03b8 + E sin \u03b8 = 0 => D cos \u03b8 + E sin \u03b8 = -r\n\nTherefore, (D/r) cos \u03b8 + (E/r) sin \u03b8 = -1\n\nTherefore, A + B + C = -1 + 1 = 0, which confirms that t = 1 is a root.\n\nTherefore, we can factor the quadratic as:\n\n(A t\u00b2 + B t + C) = (t - 1)(A t + (A + B)) ?\n\nWait, the factoring of a quadratic with root t = 1 is (t - 1)(something). Let me write:\n\nA t\u00b2 + B t + C = (t - 1)(A t + K)\n\nExpanding the right side:\n\nA t\u00b2 + K t - A t - K\n\nComparing coefficients:\n\nA t\u00b2 + (K - A) t - K = A t\u00b2 + B t + C\n\nTherefore:\n\nK - A = B\n\n- K = C\n\nFrom the second equation, K = -C\n\nSubstitute into first equation:\n\n- C - A = B => - (1 + D/r) - 2(1 - cos \u03b8) = B\n\nBut we already know that A + B + C = 0, so this is consistent.\n\nTherefore, the quadratic factors as (t - 1)(A t - C - A). Wait, maybe better to perform polynomial division.\n\nBut in any case, since we know t = 1 is a root, the other root can be found using Vieta's formula:\n\nt1 * t2 = C / A\n\nGiven that one root is t1 = 1, then the other root is t2 = C / A\n\nFrom above, C = 1 + (D/r), A = 2(1 - cos \u03b8)\n\nSo, t2 = [1 + (D/r)] / [2(1 - cos \u03b8)]\n\nRecall that D = r [ sin \u03b8 - cos(\u03b8/2) ] / cos(\u03b8/2 )\n\nSo, D/r = [ sin \u03b8 - cos(\u03b8/2) ] / cos(\u03b8/2 )\n\nTherefore,\n\n1 + D/r = 1 + [ sin \u03b8 - cos(\u03b8/2) ] / cos(\u03b8/2 ) = [ cos(\u03b8/2 ) + sin \u03b8 - cos(\u03b8/2 ) ] / cos(\u03b8/2 ) = sin \u03b8 / cos(\u03b8/2 )\n\nThus,\n\nt2 = [ sin \u03b8 / cos(\u03b8/2 ) ] / [ 2(1 - cos \u03b8) ]\n\nSimplify denominator:\n\n1 - cos \u03b8 = 2 sin\u00b2(\u03b8/2 )\n\nTherefore,\n\nt2 = [ sin \u03b8 / cos(\u03b8/2 ) ] / [ 4 sin\u00b2(\u03b8/2 ) ] = [ sin \u03b8 ] / [ 4 sin\u00b2(\u03b8/2 ) cos(\u03b8/2 ) ]\n\nBut sin \u03b8 = 2 sin(\u03b8/2) cos(\u03b8/2 )\n\nTherefore,\n\nt2 = [ 2 sin(\u03b8/2) cos(\u03b8/2) ] / [ 4 sin\u00b2(\u03b8/2 ) cos(\u03b8/2 ) ] = 2 / 4 sin(\u03b8/2 ) = 1 / (2 sin(\u03b8/2 ) )\n\nTherefore, the parameter t for point D is t = 1 / (2 sin(\u03b8/2 ) )\n\nTherefore, coordinates of D are:\n\nx = r [1 + t (cos \u03b8 - 1) ] = r [1 + (1/(2 sin(\u03b8/2 )) )(cos \u03b8 - 1) ]\n\ny = r t sin \u03b8 = r [1/(2 sin(\u03b8/2 )) ] sin \u03b8\n\nSimplify x and y.\n\nFirst, compute y:\n\ny = r [ sin \u03b8 ] / [ 2 sin(\u03b8/2 ) ] = r [ 2 sin(\u03b8/2 ) cos(\u03b8/2 ) ] / [ 2 sin(\u03b8/2 ) ] = r cos(\u03b8/2 )\n\nSo, y-coordinate of D is r cos(\u03b8/2 )\n\nNow, compute x:\n\nx = r [ 1 + (cos \u03b8 - 1)/(2 sin(\u03b8/2 )) ] = r [ (2 sin(\u03b8/2 ) + cos \u03b8 - 1 ) / (2 sin(\u03b8/2 )) ]\n\nLet me compute the numerator:\n\n2 sin(\u03b8/2 ) + cos \u03b8 - 1\n\nExpress cos \u03b8 in terms of \u03b8/2:\n\ncos \u03b8 = 1 - 2 sin\u00b2(\u03b8/2 )\n\nTherefore,\n\n2 sin(\u03b8/2 ) + (1 - 2 sin\u00b2(\u03b8/2 )) - 1 = 2 sin(\u03b8/2 ) - 2 sin\u00b2(\u03b8/2 )\n\nFactor out 2 sin(\u03b8/2 ):\n\n2 sin(\u03b8/2 )(1 - sin(\u03b8/2 ))\n\nTherefore,\n\nx = r [ 2 sin(\u03b8/2 )(1 - sin(\u03b8/2 )) ] / [ 2 sin(\u03b8/2 ) ] = r (1 - sin(\u03b8/2 ))\n\nTherefore, coordinates of D are ( r (1 - sin(\u03b8/2 )), r cos(\u03b8/2 ) )\n\nNow, we have coordinates of A(r,0), D(r (1 - sin(\u03b8/2 )), r cos(\u03b8/2 )), and O(0,0).\n\nWe need to show that triangle ADO is isosceles at A, i.e., AD = AO.\n\nCompute AO: distance from A(r,0) to O(0,0) is sqrt(r\u00b2 + 0) = r.\n\nCompute AD: distance from A(r,0) to D(r (1 - sin(\u03b8/2 )), r cos(\u03b8/2 )).\n\nCoordinates of A: (r,0)\n\nCoordinates of D: ( r (1 - sin(\u03b8/2 )), r cos(\u03b8/2 ) )\n\nTherefore,\n\nAD = sqrt[ ( r (1 - sin(\u03b8/2 )) - r )\u00b2 + ( r cos(\u03b8/2 ) - 0 )\u00b2 ]\n\nSimplify:\n\n= sqrt[ ( - r sin(\u03b8/2 ) )\u00b2 + ( r cos(\u03b8/2 ) )\u00b2 ]\n\n= sqrt[ r\u00b2 sin\u00b2(\u03b8/2 ) + r\u00b2 cos\u00b2(\u03b8/2 ) ]\n\n= sqrt[ r\u00b2 (sin\u00b2(\u03b8/2 ) + cos\u00b2(\u03b8/2 )) ]\n\n= sqrt[ r\u00b2 ] = r\n\nTherefore, AD = r = AO.\n\nTherefore, triangle ADO has AD = AO, so it is isosceles at A.\n\nHence, proved.\n\n**Final Answer**\n\\boxed{ADO \\text{ is isosceles at } A}", "deepseek_solution": "Given a circle \\(\\Gamma\\) with center \\(O\\) and points \\(A\\) and \\(B\\) on \\(\\Gamma\\) such that \\(AB > AO\\). The angle bisector of \\(\\angle OAB\\) intersects \\(\\Gamma\\) again at point \\(C\\). The circumcircle of \\(\\triangle OCB\\) intersects the line \\(AB\\) again at point \\(D\\). We need to show that \\(\\triangle ADO\\) is isosceles at \\(A\\).\n\n1. **Coordinates and Setup**:\n   - Place \\(O\\) at the origin \\((0,0)\\) and \\(A\\) at \\((r,0)\\) on the circle \\(\\Gamma\\) with radius \\(r\\).\n   - Point \\(B\\) is parametrized as \\((r \\cos \\theta, r \\sin \\theta)\\) where \\(\\theta > 60^\\circ\\) since \\(AB > AO = r\\).\n\n2. **Angle Bisector and Point \\(C\\)**:\n   - The angle bisector of \\(\\angle OAB\\) meets \\(\\Gamma\\) again at \\(C\\). Using coordinate geometry, the coordinates of \\(C\\) are found to be \\((-r \\sin(\\theta/2), r \\cos(\\theta/2))\\).\n\n3. **Circumcircle of \\(\\triangle OCB\\)**:\n   - The circumcircle \\(\\Omega\\) of \\(\\triangle OCB\\) is determined. The equation of \\(\\Omega\\) is derived using the coordinates of \\(O\\), \\(B\\), and \\(C\\).\n\n4. **Intersection of \\(\\Omega\\) with Line \\(AB\\)**:\n   - Parametric equations for line \\(AB\\) are used to find the second intersection point \\(D\\) with \\(\