O} + \\widehat{BCA}\n   \\]\n   Substituting the known values:\n   \\[\n   \\widehat{ADO} = \\frac{\\alpha}{2} + \\left( 90^\\circ - \\alpha \\right) = 90^\\circ - \\frac{\\alpha}{2}\n   \\]\n\n6. **Show triangle is isosceles:**\n   According to our goal:\n   \\[\n   \\widehat{ADO} = 90^\\circ - \\frac{\\alpha}{2}\n   \\]\n   This reveals that:\n   \\[\n   \\widehat{DAO} = \\alpha \\quad \\text{and} \\quad \\widehat{ADO} = 90^\\circ - \\frac{\\alpha}{2}\n   \\]\n   Therefore, \\( \\triangle ADO \\) is indeed isosceles at \\( A \\).\n\n### Conclusion: \nWe have successfully shown that \\( \\triangle ADO \\) is isosceles at \\( A \\).\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\widehat{ADO} = 90^\\circ - \\frac{\\alpha}{2}, \\;\\; \\triangle ADO \\text{ is isosceles at } A}\n\\]", "domain": "math", "source": "numina_math", "test_cases": null, "starter_code": null}, {"problem": "If the equation $\\frac{|x|}{x+6}=k x^{2}$ has four distinct real solutions, then the range of the real number $k$ is $\\qquad$ .", "deepseek_reasoning": "Okay, so I need to find the range of real numbers k for which the equation |x|/(x + 6) = k x\u00b2 has four distinct real solutions. Hmm, let me start by understanding the problem step by step.\n\nFirst, the equation is |x| divided by (x + 6) equals k times x squared. The absolute value in the numerator suggests that the equation will behave differently depending on whether x is positive or negative. Also, the denominator x + 6 means that x cannot be -6 because that would make the denominator zero, leading to an undefined expression. So, x \u2260 -6 is a critical point here.\n\nSince we have |x|, I should consider splitting the problem into two cases: one where x is non-negative (x \u2265 0) and another where x is negative (x < 0). This way, I can remove the absolute value and handle each case separately. Let's start with the case when x is non-negative.\n\nCase 1: x \u2265 0\n\nIn this case, |x| = x, so the equation becomes x/(x + 6) = k x\u00b2. Let me rewrite this 